OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026", "title": "A Counterexample to Kurosh’s Division-Ring Problem", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "201", "family_title": "A counterexample to Kurosh’s division-ring problem", "abstract_html": "We construct a countable division ring of characteristic zero that is algebraic over its center, generated by two elements as an algebra over that center, and infinite-dimensional over it. This gives a negative answer to the Kurosh problem for division rings.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a countable division ring \\(D\\) of characteristic zero, its center \\(F=Z(D)\\), and elements \\(x,y\\in D\\) such that \\( D=F[x,y]=F(x,y),\\qquad [D:F]=\\infty, \\) while every element of \\(D\\) is algebraic over …", "statement": "<p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">A division ring \\(D\\), with center \\(F=Z(D)\\), is algebraic over \\(F\\) if every element of \\(D\\) satisfies a nonzero polynomial in \\(F[t]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subseteq D\\), \\(F(S)\\) denotes the division subring generated by \\(F\\cup S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The division ring is locally finite if \\([F(S):F]&lt;\\infty\\) for every finite subset \\(S\\subseteq D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(F[x,y]\\) denotes the unital, not necessarily commutative, \\(F\\)-algebra generated by \\(x,y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a countable division ring \\(D\\) of characteristic zero, its center \\(F=Z(D)\\), and elements \\(x,y\\in D\\) such that\n\\[\nD=F[x,y]=F(x,y),\\qquad [D:F]=\\infty,\n\\]\nwhile every element of \\(D\\) is algebraic over \\(F\\). In particular, the pair \\(\\{x,y\\}\\) witnesses failure of local finiteness.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take \\(R,k_\\infty,x,y\\) supplied by Proposition 12, and let\n\\[\nF=Z(R).\n\\]\nThe field \\(F\\) contains \\(k_\\infty\\), and is algebraic over it because every element of \\(R\\) is. Every element of \\(R\\) is therefore algebraic over \\(F\\). Since \\(R\\) is already division,\n\\[\nR=k_\\infty[x,y]=F[x,y]=F(x,y).\n\\]</p><p>It remains to prove infinite dimension over the actual center. Suppose\n\\[\n[R:F]=n&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nChoose an \\(F\\)-basis\n\\[\nb_1=1,b_2,\\ldots,b_n\n\\]\nand write\n\\[\nb_ib_j=\\sum_{h=1}^nc_{ijh}b_h,\\qquad\nx=\\sum_{h=1}^nu_hb_h,\\qquad\ny=\\sum_{h=1}^nv_hb_h,\n\\]\nwith coefficients in \\(F\\).</p><p>Let \\(E\\) be the subfield of \\(F\\) generated over \\(k_\\infty\\) by these finitely many coefficients. Algebraicity gives\n\\[\n[E:k_\\infty]&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe space\n\\[\nB=\\sum_{h=1}^nEb_h\n\\]\ncontains \\(1,x,y\\) and is closed under multiplication by the displayed structure constants. Thus it is a \\(k_\\infty\\)-subalgebra of \\(R\\) containing\n\\[\nk_\\infty[x,y]=R.\n\\]\nHence \\(B=R\\), giving\n\\[\n[R:k_\\infty]\\leq n[E:k_\\infty]&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\ncontrary to Proposition 12.</p><p>Therefore \\([R:F]=\\infty\\). Set \\(D=R\\). It is countable, has characteristic zero, is algebraic over its actual center, and satisfies\n\\[\nD=F[x,y]=F(x,y),\\qquad [D:F]=\\infty.\n\\]\nThe finite set \\(\\{x,y\\}\\) witnesses failure of local finiteness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following evaluation rules hold.", "statement": "<p>Formal evaluations and detection by independent residues; this collects the evaluation conventions of Section 4.1.</p><p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a field of characteristic zero, let \\(A\\) be a finite-dimensional central division algebra over \\(k\\), and fix an algebraic closure \\(K\\) of \\(k\\) and a splitting\n\\[\nA\\otimes_k K\\simeq M_d(K),\\qquad d=\\deg A.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n\\[\nw=\\sum_\\omega a_\\omega z^\\omega\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe free letters commute with \\(A\\), but not with each other.</p><p class=\"li\">For an \\(n\\times n\\) scalar matrix pair \\(P=(P_1,P_2)\\), put\n\\[\nW_n(P)=\\sum_\\omega a_\\omega\\otimes P^\\omega.\n\\]\nThe coefficient matrices act on the first tensor factor; input matrices act on the second.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be a complete discretely valued field containing \\(K\\), with valuation \\(v\\) trivial on \\(K^\\times\\). A matrix is small if the minimum valuation of its entries is positive. We use a parameter \\(\\varepsilon\\) with \\(v(\\varepsilon)=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following evaluation rules hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If a constant input pair \\(R\\) is block upper triangular with small diagonal blocks, then \\(W(R+H)\\), for a positive-order commuting or free formal perturbation \\(H\\), is defined coefficientwise by convergent sums. Such evaluations preserve products and formal entry identities, including fixed-order derivatives and finite determinants.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. These rules remain valid coefficientwise in an additional parameter \\(\\eta\\), with diagonal entries in \\(E[[\\eta]]\\) having small constant terms and with strictly upper entries and perturbation coefficients in \\(E((\\eta))\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. A nonzero formal series whose coefficients have valuations uniformly bounded below remains nonzero when evaluated at \\(\\varepsilon\\) times variables with algebraically independent residues.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Rational identities involving finite matrix inverses are transferred by first clearing determinants, evaluating the resulting numerators, and dividing only when the evaluated determinants are nonzero. Formal inverse expansions are not presumed to converge at every specialization.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Evaluation at a small generic point embeds \\(K[[P]]\\), and hence its fraction field, into a suitable Laurent field. Taylor substitution at a small block-diagonal point is injective. Taylor substitution\n\\[\nP\\longmapsto P+\\eta v\n\\]\nextends uniquely, with prescribed reduction, through any finite separable extension of \\(\\operatorname{Frac}K[[P]]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The convergence and identity assertions are Lemma 1.1. Detection is Lemma 1.2.</p><p>For an inverse, first multiply an identity by its determinant. The cleared numerator is a finite sum of products of base-point scalars and matrix-entry series. The path estimate gives a uniform valuation bound in the auxiliary coordinates. Lemmas 1.1 and 1.2 therefore transfer the numerator identity; division is performed only when the specialized determinant is nonzero. The base-point valued field is constructed first, and auxiliary residues are adjoined afterward, preserving independence after the base spectrum has been split. The argument applies separately at each Taylor degree.</p><p>The evaluation\n\\[\nK[[P]]\\longrightarrow C(P^\\circ)((\\varepsilon)),\n\\qquad P\\longmapsto\\varepsilon P^\\circ,\n\\]\nis injective by homogeneous degree, or by Lemma 1.2. It extends to fraction fields, and a finite splitting field embeds into a finite algebraic extension of the target.</p><p>If \\(R\\) is small, the map\n\\[\nK[[P]]\\longrightarrow E[[\\Delta]],\n\\qquad P\\longmapsto R+\\Delta,\n\\]\nis injective: reduction modulo the valuation ideal sends the image of \\(f\\) to \\(f(\\Delta)\\). Nonzero denominators remain nonzero.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nP\\longmapsto P+\\eta v\n\\]\nmaps \\(\\operatorname{Frac}K[[P]]\\) into\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Frac}K[[P]][[\\eta]]\n\\]\nwith reduction the identity. For a finite separable extension \\(\\Omega\\), choose a primitive element and lift its defining equation using its nonzero derivative. Coefficient recursion gives the unique lift reducing to that primitive element. This transports labeled spectral branches while the base point moves.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Admissible evaluations.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The evaluation \\(W(R+H)\\) is defined coefficientwise by convergent sums in \\(E\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, suppose a constant input pair \\(R\\) is block upper triangular with \\(b\\) blocks and small diagonal blocks. Its strictly upper blocks may have arbitrary entries in \\(E\\). Let \\(H\\) be a matrix pair of positive-order formal series in finitely many commuting or free variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The evaluation \\(W(R+H)\\) is defined coefficientwise by convergent sums in \\(E\\). The same assertion holds for products, finite determinants, and derivatives of fixed order of finitely many such entry series. These evaluations preserve formal entry identities.</p><p>The assertions also hold coefficientwise in an additional parameter \\(\\eta\\) if the diagonal blocks have entries in \\(E[[\\eta]]\\) with small constant terms, while the strictly upper blocks and the coefficients of \\(H\\) have entries in \\(E((\\eta))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a coefficient of degree \\(r\\) in the variables of \\(H\\). An input path contributing to this coefficient uses at most \\(r\\) positive-order terms of \\(H\\). Between these insertions it follows the constant upper triangular pair. In each of the at most \\(r+1\\) resulting intervals, there are at most \\(b-1\\) strictly upper steps. Thus at most\n\\[\n(r+1)(b-1)\n\\]\nconstant factors are strictly upper. All remaining constant factors come from small diagonal blocks.</p><p>Only finitely many coefficients of \\(H\\) can occur at the chosen degree. Their valuations, and those of the finitely many strictly upper blocks, have a common lower bound. If the original word has length \\(L\\), its contribution consequently has valuation at least\n\\[\ncL-C_r,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&gt;0\\) and \\(C_r\\) is independent of the word. There are finitely many words of each length, so the sum converges.</p><p>The same estimate applies after inserting the finitely many extra factors needed for a product or a derivative of fixed order. Completeness allows regrouping of these sums, proving compatibility with multiplication and identities.</p><p>For the additional parameter, fix both the degree \\(r\\) in \\(H\\) and an \\(\\eta\\)-coefficient. The bounded number of strictly upper steps and \\(H\\)-insertions bounds their total negative \\(\\eta\\)-order. Hence only a bounded number of positive-order diagonal \\(\\eta\\)-terms can contribute, and only finitely many of their coefficients are used. Every other factor is a small constant diagonal term. The preceding estimate therefore applies again. This is a coefficientwise assertion; no interchange of the two Laurent topologies is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, “Detection by independent residues.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The evaluation \\(F(\\varepsilon Q^\\circ)\\) is defined, and it is nonzero whenever \\(F\\) is nonzero.", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\nF(Q)=\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha Q^\\alpha\\in E[[Q_1,\\ldots,Q_N]],\n\\qquad \\inf_\\alpha v(c_\\alpha)&gt;-\\infty.\n\\]\nAdjoin \\(Q_1^\\circ,\\ldots,Q_N^\\circ\\) whose residues are algebraically independent over the residue field of \\(E\\), and complete for the extended valuation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The evaluation \\(F(\\varepsilon Q^\\circ)\\) is defined, and it is nonzero whenever \\(F\\) is nonzero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The valuations\n\\[\nv(c_\\alpha)+|\\alpha|\n\\]\ntend uniformly to infinity with \\(|\\alpha|\\). Their least value is therefore attained by a nonempty finite set of monomials. After scaling to this valuation, the residue of their sum is a nonzero polynomial in the independent residues of the \\(Q_j^\\circ\\). This proves both assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The algebra \\(A\\otimes_k B_n\\) is division, and remains division under pure rational and central Laurent scalar extensions.", "statement": "<p>Generic division evaluations and stable spectra; this collects Section 4.2–4.3.</p><p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) have characteristic zero and contain every root of unity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/k\\) be a central division algebra of degree \\(d\\), and let\n\\[\nw\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle.\n\\]\nUse the splitting and evaluation conventions of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q_0\\in k[t]\\) be the minimal polynomial of \\(w(0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(n\\geq1\\), take independent central variables \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\), a primitive \\(n\\)-th root \\(\\zeta_n\\in k\\), and the symbol algebra\n\\[\nB_n=k(\\alpha,\\beta)\\langle x,y\\rangle/\n(x^n-\\alpha,\\ y^n-\\beta,\\ yx-\\zeta_nxy).\n\\]\nFor \\(n=1\\), set \\(B_1=k(\\alpha,\\beta)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a small generic scalar matrix input of size \\(n\\), let \\(e_n\\) be the minimal-polynomial degree of \\(W_n\\), and put \\(t_n=dn/e_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The algebra \\(A\\otimes_k B_n\\) is division, and remains division under pure rational and central Laurent scalar extensions. Generic \\(W_n\\) is semisimple after splitting, and every distinct eigenvalue has the same positive integer multiplicity \\(t_n\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\), there exists \\(n_0\\) such that, for every \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every distinct generic eigenvalue of \\(W_n\\) has nonzero differential in the input entries. Independent small generic points of sizes at least \\(n_0\\) have disjoint spectra, and these spectra avoid that of \\(w(0)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Choose \\(p\\geq n_0\\) minimizing \\(t_p\\), set \\(t=t_p\\), and put\n\\[\nS=\\{pN:N\\geq1\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nt_{pN}=t,\\qquad e_{pN}=Ne_p.\n\\]\nAt every admissible specialization of size \\(n\\in S\\), the minimal-polynomial degree is at most \\(e_n=dn/t\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. At a commuting Taylor deformation whose constant output is split semisimple with \\(e_n\\) distinct roots, formal conjugation separates the output into scalar blocks, with their constant multiplicities.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Let \\(\\rho_n\\) be the generic rank of the input Jacobian of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}W_n,\\operatorname{tr}W_n^2,\\ldots,\\operatorname{tr}W_n^{dn}.\n\\]\nIts minors give specialization-stable rank bounds. At every maximal-root-count point, the trace and eigenvalue Jacobians have the same rank.</p><p>If \\(q_0(w)=0\\), the later spectral correction is unnecessary; the division-realization assertions remain available.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies the division models.</p><p>In the division evaluation of Lemma 2.2, let \\(e_n\\) be the minimal-polynomial degree. The minimal polynomial is irreducible and separable. Hence \\(W_n(P)\\) is semisimple after splitting, with equal root multiplicities.</p><p>More explicitly, if \\(D/F\\) has degree \\(N\\) and \\(a\\in D\\) has minimal polynomial \\(q\\) of degree \\(e\\), left multiplication by \\(a\\) has characteristic polynomial\n\\[\nq^{N^2/e},\n\\]\nby viewing \\(D\\) as an \\(F[a]\\)-vector space. After splitting \\(D=M_N\\), left multiplication acts by the matrix \\(a\\) on each of \\(N\\) columns. Comparing root multiplicities gives \\(N/e\\) in the matrix representation, proving that this is an integer.</p><p>After splitting \\(A,B_n\\), the \\(b_j\\) become a basis of \\(M_n\\). The change from \\(c_{ij}\\) to the entries of \\(P_i/\\varepsilon\\) is invertible. Thus these are small generic scalar evaluations. Linear independence of\n\\[\n1,a,\\ldots,a^{e_n-1}\n\\]\nis preserved by field extension, so the same \\(e_n,t_n\\) describe the generic formal matrix evaluation.</p><p>Lemmas 2.2 and 2.3 give nonconstant roots, disjointness, and the progression \\(S\\). The same vectorization-minor identities bound the minimal-polynomial degree at all admissible specializations. Lemma 2.4 supplies the scalar Taylor branches.</p><p>At a maximal-root-count point, write the distinct roots as \\(\\lambda_h\\), each with multiplicity \\(t\\). Then\n\\[\nd\\,\\operatorname{tr}W^j\n=jt\\sum_{h=1}^{e_n}\\lambda_h^{j-1}\\,d\\lambda_h.\n\\]\nThe first \\(e_n\\) rows form an invertible Vandermonde matrix. Hence the trace and eigenvalue Jacobians have the same rank, including over a commuting Taylor fraction field. Formal minors of the trace Jacobian supply the stated specialized rank bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(A\\) and \\(B_n\\) as in Proposition 2, the central simple algebra \\(A\\otimes_k B_n\\) is division.", "statement": "<p>For \\(A\\) and \\(B_n\\) as in Proposition 2, the central simple algebra \\(A\\otimes_k B_n\\) is division. Its pure rational scalar extensions and central Laurent series extensions are division as well.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with \\(A((x))\\), where \\(x\\) commutes with \\(A\\), and let \\(\\sigma\\) fix \\(A\\) and send \\(x\\) to \\(\\zeta_nx\\). The skew Laurent series ring\n\\[\nA((x))((y;\\sigma))\n\\]\nis a division ring by the leading-coefficient construction of inverses. The elements \\(x^n,y^n\\) are central and algebraically independent.</p><p>The defining relations give a unital map from \\(A\\otimes_k B_n\\) into this division ring. The reduced monomials \\(x^iy^j\\), \\(0\\leq i,j&lt;n\\), form a basis of \\(B_n\\): they span by the relations, and their clock-and-shift matrices after adjoining roots of \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are linearly independent and span \\(M_n\\). Thus \\(B_n\\) is central simple, and central simplicity makes the map injective.</p><p>A finite-dimensional domain over a field is division, since multiplication by a nonzero element is injective and hence surjective.</p><p>For a central division algebra \\(D/F\\), the rational extension \\(D(t)\\) embeds in \\(D((t))\\). Iterating proves the rational assertion. Since \\(D\\) has a finite \\(F\\)-basis,\n\\[\nD\\otimes_F F((\\varepsilon))=D((\\varepsilon)),\n\\]\nwhich is division by leading coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every distinct generic eigenvalue of \\(W_n(P)\\) has a nonzero differential in the input entries.", "statement": "<p>Assume \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\). Choose \\(n_0\\) such that\n\\[\nq_0(W_n(P))\\neq0\n\\]\nat every generic size \\(n\\geq n_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every distinct generic eigenvalue of \\(W_n(P)\\) has a nonzero differential in the input entries. Independent small generic points of sizes at least \\(n_0\\) have disjoint spectra, and their spectra avoid the constant spectrum of \\(w(0)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A nonzero homogeneous part of \\(q_0(w)\\) is detected by scalar matrices of every sufficiently large size. Choose a word of length \\(L\\) with nonzero coefficient and use a path on \\(L+1\\) vertices, placing its successive letters on consecutive matrix-unit arrows. The \\((1,L+1)\\)-entry detects precisely that coefficient. Padding by zero blocks gives the required \\(n_0\\).</p><p>Choose a basis \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_{n^2}\\) of \\(B_n\\) and independent variables \\(c_{ij}\\). Evaluate\n\\[\nP_i=\\varepsilon\\sum_j c_{ij}b_j\n\\]\ninside a central division algebra of degree \\(dn\\) over\n\\[\nF_n=k(\\alpha,\\beta,(c_{ij}))((\\varepsilon)).\n\\]\nEach derivation \\(\\partial/\\partial c_{ij}\\) acts coefficientwise on Laurent series and extends uniquely to algebraic extensions. Uniqueness makes it commute with automorphisms of a splitting field over \\(F_n\\).</p><p>If all these derivations killed one root, they would kill all its Galois conjugates. Irreducibility makes these all the distinct roots. Thus every coefficient of the reduced characteristic polynomial would have all its input derivatives zero.</p><p>The coefficient of \\(\\varepsilon^r\\) in such a characteristic coefficient is a homogeneous polynomial of degree \\(r\\) in the \\(c_{ij}\\). In characteristic zero, a polynomial whose partial derivatives all vanish is constant. All positive-\\(\\varepsilon\\)-degree terms therefore vanish. The characteristic polynomial is that of \\(w(0)\\). Semisimplicity then implies\n\\[\nq_0(W_n(P))=0,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>Splitting \\(A,B_n\\) uses constants independent of the inputs. The split basis change from \\(c_{ij}\\) to matrix entries, together with the nonzero factor \\(\\varepsilon\\), transports the differential assertion to ordinary matrix-entry coordinates.</p><p>For independent points \\(P,Q\\), derivations in the \\(P\\)-coordinates kill roots belonging to \\(Q\\), but cannot all kill a root belonging to \\(P\\). Thus the spectra are disjoint. The same argument excludes a constant root.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(N\\geq1\\), \\( t_{pN}=t,\\qquad e_{pN}=Ne_p. \\)", "statement": "<p>Under the spectral assumptions of Proposition 2, choose \\(p\\geq n_0\\) for which \\(t_p\\) is minimal, and put \\(t=t_p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(N\\geq1\\),\n\\[\nt_{pN}=t,\\qquad e_{pN}=Ne_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Specialize a size-\\(pN\\) point to a diagonal sum of \\(N\\) independent size-\\(p\\) points. Lemma 2.2 gives \\(Ne_p\\) distinct eigenvalues there.</p><p>The generic minimal-polynomial-degree bound survives specialization: it is expressed by vanishing of all \\((e_{pN}+1)\\)-minors of the matrix whose columns are vectorizations of\n\\[\n1,W,\\ldots,W^{e_{pN}}.\n\\]\nThese are formal entry identities covered by Proposition 1. Consequently\n\\[\ne_{pN}\\geq Ne_p.\n\\]\nMinimality of \\(t\\) gives the opposite inequality:\n\\[\ne_{pN}=\\frac{dpN}{t_{pN}}\n\\leq\\frac{dpN}{t}=Ne_p.\n\\]\nBoth assertions follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.6, “Scalar branches at maximal root count.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A formal change of basis reducing to the identity separates \\(V(T)\\) into \\(e\\) scalar blocks.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V(T)\\) be a matrix over a commuting formal Taylor ring \\(E[[T]]\\). Suppose:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(V(0)\\) is split semisimple with \\(e\\) distinct eigenvalues;</p><p class=\"li\">the minimal-polynomial degree of \\(V(T)\\) over \\(\\operatorname{Frac}E[[T]]\\) is at most \\(e\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A formal change of basis reducing to the identity separates \\(V(T)\\) into \\(e\\) scalar blocks. Their sizes are the multiplicities at \\(T=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the eigenspace decomposition at zero. Off-diagonal blocks can be removed recursively in total Taylor degree: the equation for the block between \\(\\lambda_i,\\lambda_j\\) is multiplication by the nonzero scalar \\(\\lambda_i-\\lambda_j\\).</p><p>The resulting blocks \\(V_i(T)\\) reduce to \\(\\lambda_iI\\). Their characteristic polynomials have pairwise unit resultants, since their reductions have different roots. Their minimal polynomials over the Taylor fraction field are therefore pairwise coprime.</p><p>The minimal-polynomial degree of their direct sum is the sum of their degrees. There are \\(e\\) nonempty blocks and the total degree is at most \\(e\\), so every block has minimal-polynomial degree one. Thus each \\(V_i(T)\\) is scalar, and its scalar entry belongs to \\(E[[T]]\\), since its entries do.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 5.3. The proposition also retains the response and specialization conclusions used in its proof", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every loop compression and paired compression above is scalar.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Work in the spectral case \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\) of Proposition 2, with progression \\(S\\), multiplicity \\(t\\), and \\(e_n=dn/t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a generic input \\(P\\) of size \\(n\\in S\\), and let \\(\\Omega_P\\) be a splitting field over \\(\\operatorname{Frac}K[[P]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write its roots as \\(\\lambda_h\\) and its rank-\\(t\\) spectral projections as \\(\\pi_h\\), \\(1\\leq h\\leq e_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a perturbation of the output, put \\(\\bar\\pi_h=1-\\pi_h\\) and define the fixed-root Schur expression\n\\[\nS_h(W)=\\pi_h(W-\\lambda_h)\\pi_h\n-\\pi_hW\\bar\\pi_h\n\\bigl(\\bar\\pi_h(W-\\lambda_h)\\bar\\pi_h\\bigr)^{-1}\n\\bar\\pi_hW\\pi_h.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an input direction \\(Y=(Y_1,Y_2)\\), define \\(D_h(P;Y)\\) by\n\\[\n\\pi_hW'_P(Y)\\pi_h=D_h(P;Y)\\pi_h.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an independent generic partner \\(Q\\) of size \\(n'\\in S\\), and scalar input-arrow pairs \\(U:P\\leftarrow Q\\), \\(V:Q\\leftarrow P\\), form\n\\[\nT=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nP&amp;U&amp;0\\\\\n0&amp;Q&amp;V\\\\\n0&amp;0&amp;P\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nM_h(P,Q;U,V)=\n\\pi_h\\left(\nW(T)_{13}\n-W(T)_{12}(W(Q)-\\lambda_h)^{-1}W(T)_{23}\n\\right)\\pi_h.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(M_h=m_h\\pi_h\\), let \\(m=(m_h)_h\\), and let \\(D=D(P;\\cdot)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Expand \\(m\\) formally in the entries of \\(Q\\) at \\(Q=0\\). Define \\(T_P\\subseteq\\Omega_P^{e_n}\\) as the span of the columns of \\(D\\) and every coefficient vector of \\(m\\), for all partner sizes in \\(S\\) and coordinate arrows \\(U,V\\). Set \\(a_n=\\dim_{\\Omega_P}T_P\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every loop compression and paired compression above is scalar. The scalar \\(m_h\\) is the mixed coefficient of the branch reducing to \\(\\lambda_h\\) for\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}P&amp;xU\\\\yV&amp;Q\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Scalarity and all linear containment identities defining \\(T_P\\) persist under admissible partner specializations whenever the required resolvents exist. For a subset of labels, only its resolvents are required.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. After scalar extension, finitely many loop columns and paired responses at independent small generic partners span \\(T_P\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Choose a constant input direction \\(v\\) with\n\\[\nD_h(P;v)\\neq0\\quad(1\\leq h\\leq e_n).\n\\]\nThere is a partition \\(\\mathcal P_P\\) of the labels such that\n\\[\nT_P=\n\\left\\{\nc\\in\\Omega_P^{e_n}:\n\\frac{c_h}{D_h(P;v)}\n\\text{ is constant on each part of }\\mathcal P_P\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nLabels in one part are called linked. The number of parts is \\(a_n\\), and projection onto one representative of each part identifies \\(T_P\\) with a full coordinate space.</p>", "proof": "<p>The loop compression is scalar by applying Proposition 2 to the commuting deformation \\(P+xY\\). It is the differential of the corresponding branch. Each \\(D_h\\) is nonzero. Since \\(K\\) is infinite, a constant direction \\(v\\) avoiding the finitely many kernels satisfies\n\\[\nD_h(P;v)\\neq0\n\\]\nfor all labels.</p><p>Lemma 3.1 proves paired scalarity, and Lemma 3.2 proves specialization and finite spanning.</p><p>It remains to prove the partition assertion. First prove closure of \\(T_P\\) under\n\\[\n(a,b)\\longmapsto\n\\left(\\frac{a_hb_h}{D_h(P;v)}\\right)_h.\n\\]\nChoose two finite spanning sets from Lemma 3.2 with mutually independent partners. It suffices to take a column \\(a\\) from one and \\(b\\) from the other, over their joint field \\(E\\).</p><p>Put \\(P'=P+\\xi v\\). Form a triangular chain with endpoints \\(P,P\\), middle point \\(P'\\), an optional partner \\(Q_1\\) before \\(P'\\), and an optional partner \\(Q_2\\) after it. A direct step represents a loop column; an excursion through \\(Q_i\\) represents a paired column. Put the arrows defining \\(a,b\\) on these successive steps. Group the internal positions as one partner \\(C(\\xi)\\), whose size belongs to \\(S\\).</p><p>For each target root, \\(W(C(\\xi))-\\lambda_h\\) is invertible over \\(E((\\xi))\\). Optional blocks have spectra disjoint from \\(P\\). At \\(P'\\), the branch reducing to \\(\\lambda_h\\) moves with nonzero derivative. Scalar branch separation gives\n\\[\n\\left.\n\\xi\\bigl(W(P')-\\lambda_h\\bigr)^{-1}\n\\right|_{\\xi=0}\n=\n\\frac{\\pi_h}{D_h(P;v)}.\n\\]</p><p>By Lemma 3.2, the paired response through \\(C(\\xi)\\) lies in \\(T_P\\otimes E((\\xi))\\). Eliminate optional blocks first. Their elimination leaves the middle diagonal block unchanged. All terms are regular except its inverse. The two effective couplings, compressed at zero, are \\(a_h\\pi_h\\) and \\(b_h\\pi_h\\), by the loop and paired formulas.</p><p>The singular term has the minus sign of the Schur complement. Multiplying by \\(\\xi\\) and reducing gives\n\\[\n\\left(-\\frac{a_hb_h}{D_h(P;v)}\\right)_h\\in T_P\\otimes E.\n\\]\nTaking a Laurent coefficient preserves membership in this fixed finite-dimensional space, as its annihilating functionals show. Bilinearity and faithful scalar extension prove the closure assertion over \\(\\Omega_P\\).</p><p>Divide coordinates by \\(D(P;v)\\). The resulting space \\(U\\subseteq\\Omega_P^{e_n}\\) contains \\(1\\) and is closed under coordinatewise multiplication. Declare coordinates equivalent when every element of \\(U\\) has equal values on them. Avoiding finitely many proper linear subspaces gives an element whose values distinguish the equivalence classes. Lagrange interpolation produces their indicator functions. These span all class-constant functions, proving the stated partition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, there exists a scalar \\(m_h(P,Q;U,V)\\) such that \\( M_h(P,Q;U,V)=m_h(P,Q;U,V)\\pi_h. \\) It is the coefficient of \\(xy\\) in the scalar eigenvalue branch reducing to \\(\\lambda_h\\) for …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3, there exists a scalar \\(m_h(P,Q;U,V)\\) such that\n\\[\nM_h(P,Q;U,V)=m_h(P,Q;U,V)\\pi_h.\n\\]\nIt is the coefficient of \\(xy\\) in the scalar eigenvalue branch reducing to \\(\\lambda_h\\) for\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nP&amp;xU\\\\\nyV&amp;Q\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p><p>The same assertion holds at an admissible specialized pair with disjoint spectra whose combined output is split semisimple with the maximal number \\(e_{n+n'}\\) of distinct roots.</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(x=y=0\\), disjointness and Proposition 2 give\n\\[\ne_n+e_{n'}=e_{n+n'}\n\\]\nroots. The scalar-branch conclusion of Proposition 2 applies. Either arrow alone leaves the spectrum unchanged, so the chosen branch has no term depending on \\(x\\) alone or \\(y\\) alone.</p><p>Compute the \\(xy\\)-coefficient of its fixed-root Schur expression. The direct return from \\(P\\) through \\(Q\\) gives \\(W(T)_{13}\\). Eliminating the \\(Q\\)-block gives the second term defining \\(M_h\\). The other eigenspaces in \\(P\\) give no further term of this order: their couplings to \\(\\pi_h\\) have zero constant term and no first-order term under these off-diagonal perturbations.</p><p>Replacing the fixed root by the scalar branch subtracts its \\(xy\\)-coefficient times \\(\\pi_h\\). The resulting Schur expression vanishes, since the branch eigenspace projects isomorphically to the base eigenspace. This proves the formula.</p><p>The specialized assertion uses the same argument and the specialization-stable degree bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Specialization and finite witnesses.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3:", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The scalar identities for \\(M_h\\), and every linear identity expressing \\(m\\in T_P\\), hold after any admissible partner specialization for which the required inverses exist. For identities on a subset of labels, only the corresponding inverses are needed.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. After a field extension, the columns of \\(D\\) and finitely many paired responses span \\(T_P\\). These responses may be taken at independent small generic partner points, whose sizes need not be equal.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply an \\(\\Omega_P\\)-linear functional annihilating \\(T_P\\) to the formal tuple \\(m\\). The result is zero. Multiply by the finitely many determinants of \\(W(Q)-\\lambda_h\\) occurring in it.</p><p>The cleared expression is a finite sum of products of base-point scalars and matrix-entry series, with auxiliary coefficients uniformly bounded below in valuation. Proposition 1 identifies its formal identity with its identity at a small generic partner and preserves it under admissible specializations. Divide by the specialized determinants when they are nonzero.</p><p>The same argument, entry by entry, proves the scalar identities for \\(M_h\\). It also proves the projected assertion without introducing spectral labels for the partner.</p><p>Choose finitely many coefficient vectors, together with loop columns, spanning \\(T_P\\), and a nonzero minor witnessing their rank. Use a separate set of partner variables for each column requiring a partner. By multilinearity, the determinant of the corresponding series columns has the chosen nonzero minor as the coefficient of a specified product of monomials. Thus it is nonzero.</p><p>Clear the column denominators. The numerator remains nonzero, with coefficients uniformly bounded below. Independent small generic evaluations keep numerator and denominators nonzero by Proposition 1. The resulting responses attain the full dimension; the first assertion puts them in the extended active space.</p><p>The proof evaluates cleared numerators, not possibly divergent geometric expansions of inverses. It applies after adjoining Taylor or Laurent parameters by first clearing determinants and then working coefficientwise. In particular, it permits triangular partners with repeated base blocks when the required target resolvents exist in the chosen Laurent field.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 6.2, “Transfer of a leading coefficient.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If every \\(S_h(Z)\\), \\(h\\in I\\), vanishes in degrees below \\(\\ell\\geq1\\), then for each word \\(\\omega\\) of length \\(\\ell\\), \\( [S_h(Z)]_\\omega=c_{h,\\omega}\\pi_h, \\qquad (c_{h,\\omega})_{h\\in I}\\in(T_P|_I)\\otimes E. \\) …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Work with the spectral data and active spaces of Propositions 2–3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(P,C_0)\n\\]\nbe a small input of total size \\(N\\in S\\), with \\(C_0\\) possibly absent.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I\\) be a set of labels at \\(P\\) such that \\(W(C_0)-\\lambda_h\\) is invertible for every \\(h\\in I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) contain the labeled spectral data at \\(P\\) and the entries of \\(C_0\\), with evaluations permitted by Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nZ(s_1,s_2)\\in M_N(E)\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^2\n\\]\nhave constant term \\(R\\). Input coefficients act on the input tensor factor and commute with \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Embed \\(\\pi_h\\) in the \\(P\\)-block, set \\(\\bar\\pi_h=1-\\pi_h\\), and define\n\\[\nS_h(Z)=\\pi_h(W(Z)-\\lambda_h)\\pi_h\n-\\pi_hW(Z)\\bar\\pi_h\n\\bigl(\\bar\\pi_h(W(Z)-\\lambda_h)\\bar\\pi_h\\bigr)^{-1}\n\\bar\\pi_hW(Z)\\pi_h.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nT_P|_I=\\{(c_h)_{h\\in I}:(c_h)_h\\in T_P\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If every \\(S_h(Z)\\), \\(h\\in I\\), vanishes in degrees below \\(\\ell\\geq1\\), then for each word \\(\\omega\\) of length \\(\\ell\\),\n\\[\n[S_h(Z)]_\\omega=c_{h,\\omega}\\pi_h,\n\\qquad\n(c_{h,\\omega})_{h\\in I}\\in(T_P|_I)\\otimes E.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds for homogeneous degree-\\(\\ell\\) coefficients of perturbations in finitely many ordinary commuting parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\ell=1\\), only the \\(P\\)-to-\\(P\\) input coefficient contributes to the compressed derivative, so Proposition 3 gives the conclusion. Assume \\(\\ell\\geq2\\), and fix a word \\(\\omega\\) of length \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>Unfold \\(\\omega\\) along \\(\\ell+1\\) ordered states, putting its letters on consecutive steps. A coefficient of \\(Z\\) for a substring becomes the corresponding strictly upper input block; every diagonal input is \\(R\\). The \\((1,\\ell+1)\\)-block extracts the coefficient of \\(\\omega\\). This substitution is a homomorphism modulo degree \\(\\ell+1\\), and respects every inverse with invertible constant term.</p><p>Keep only \\(P\\) at the two endpoints, retaining all \\(R\\)-blocks internally. This does not change the compressed endpoint coefficient: constant endpoint steps preserve \\(P,C_0\\), and the constant output commutes with \\(\\pi_h\\). A Schur path cannot enter the first endpoint complement from the target projection or leave the last endpoint complement for it. All relevant complement eliminations occur internally.</p><p>Write the cropped input as\n\\[\nT_0=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nP&amp;G_1&amp;Y\\\\\n0&amp;Q_0&amp;G_2\\\\\n0&amp;0&amp;P\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q_0\\) has size \\((\\ell-1)N\\in S\\). Put\n\\[\nE_1=W(T_0)_{12},\\qquad\nE_2=W(T_0)_{23},\\qquad\nF=W(T_0)_{13}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(h\\in I\\), set \\(M_0=W(Q_0)-\\lambda_h\\), and let \\(e\\) be the sum of the internal fixed copies of \\(\\pi_h\\); put \\(f=1-e\\). The matrix \\(fM_0f\\) is upper triangular with invertible diagonal blocks. Its Schur complement on \\(eV\\) is zero: every strictly upper coefficient is a proper-substring coefficient and vanishes by hypothesis.</p><p>Consequently its kernel is the graph\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto u-f(fM_0f)^{-1}fM_0eu,\\qquad u\\in eV.\n\\]\nProjection onto \\(eV\\) is an isomorphism on the kernel, whose dimension is \\((\\ell-1)t\\). Triangularity gives the same algebraic zero multiplicity, so zero is semisimple. Let \\(\\Pi\\) be its spectral projection.</p><p>Apply the same argument to the chains consisting of one endpoint and the internal states. They involve only proper substrings. Their triangular kernel equations imply\n\\[\n\\pi_hE_1\\Pi=0,\\qquad \\Pi E_2\\pi_h=0:\n\\]\na nonzero compressed coupling would reduce the kernel dimension below the sum of the diagonal zero multiplicities.</p><p>In every internal copy of \\(R\\), replace \\(P\\) by \\(P+\\xi v\\), where\n\\[\nD_h(P;v)\\neq0\n\\]\nfor all target labels, leaving the arrows fixed. Denote the partner by \\(Q(\\xi)\\), the full input by \\(T(\\xi)\\), and the corresponding output blocks by \\(E_1(\\xi),E_2(\\xi),F(\\xi)\\).</p><p>The spectral projection \\(\\Pi\\) and its graph identification are upper triangular in state order. Thus\n\\[\n\\left.\\Pi M'(0)\\Pi\\right|_{\\ker M_0},\n\\qquad M(\\xi)=W(Q(\\xi))-\\lambda_h,\n\\]\nis upper triangular with every diagonal block \\(D_h(P;v)I_t\\). It is invertible, verifying Lemma 4.1.</p><p>Over \\(E((\\xi))\\), the endpoint expression is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_h(\\xi)\n&amp;=\\pi_h\\bigl(F(\\xi)-E_1(\\xi)M(\\xi)^{-1}E_2(\\xi)\\bigr)\\pi_h\\\\\n&amp;=D_h(P;Y)\\pi_h+M_h(P,Q(\\xi);G_1,G_2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIts tuple is scalar and lies in the projected active space by Proposition 3. To apply specialization, clear the target resolvent determinants first; Proposition 1 then evaluates the triangular partner coefficientwise. The preceding argument restores its inverses over \\(E((\\xi))\\).</p><p>Lemma 4.1 gives the finite limit\n\\[\n\\pi_h(F-E_1JE_2)\\pi_h\n=\n\\pi_h\\bigl(F-E_1f(fM_0f)^{-1}fE_2\\bigr)\\pi_h.\n\\]\nThis eliminates precisely the fixed internal complementary spaces. By unfolding and endpoint cropping, it is \\([S_h(Z)]_\\omega\\). Taking the limit in scalarity and in the finitely many linear equations defining \\(T_P|_I\\) proves the free-word assertion.</p><p>For commuting parameters \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_s\\), substitute \\(h_j=a_jz\\) for arbitrary scalar directions \\(a_j\\) and one free letter \\(z\\). The result just proved applies to degree \\(\\ell\\) on every such line. Scalarity and membership in a fixed finite-dimensional space are linear conditions; their annihilators give homogeneous polynomials in the \\(a_j\\) vanishing identically. Over the infinite characteristic-zero field, all coefficients vanish. This proves the commuting assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “An inverse between two vanishing couplings.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The inverse \\(M(\\xi)^{-1}\\) exists over \\(E((\\xi))\\), has a pole of order at most one, and \\( \\lim_{\\xi\\to0}L(\\xi)M(\\xi)^{-1}B(\\xi)=L(0)JB(0). \\) If additionally \\(e\\) is an idempotent, \\(f=1-e\\), \\(fM_0f\\) is …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be a field, \\(V\\) a finite-dimensional \\(E\\)-vector space, and\n\\[\nM(\\xi)\\in\\operatorname{End}_E(V)[[\\xi]].\n\\]\nSuppose zero is a semisimple eigenvalue of \\(M_0=M(0)\\), with spectral projection \\(\\Pi\\), and\n\\[\nG=\\left.\\Pi M'(0)\\Pi\\right|_{\\ker M_0}\n\\]\nis invertible.</p><p>Let \\(J\\) be zero on \\(\\ker M_0\\) and equal to \\(M_0^{-1}\\) on \\(\\operatorname{im}M_0\\). Let compatible matrix series \\(L(\\xi),B(\\xi)\\) satisfy\n\\[\nL(0)\\Pi=0,\\qquad \\Pi B(0)=0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The inverse \\(M(\\xi)^{-1}\\) exists over \\(E((\\xi))\\), has a pole of order at most one, and\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\xi\\to0}L(\\xi)M(\\xi)^{-1}B(\\xi)=L(0)JB(0).\n\\]</p><p>If additionally \\(e\\) is an idempotent, \\(f=1-e\\), \\(fM_0f\\) is invertible on \\(fV\\), and\n\\[\ne:\\ker M_0\\longrightarrow eV\n\\]\nis an isomorphism, then\n\\[\nL(0)JB(0)=L(0)f(fM_0f)^{-1}fB(0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(V=\\ker M_0\\oplus\\operatorname{im}M_0\\). Then\n\\[\nM(\\xi)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\xi G+O(\\xi^2)&amp;O(\\xi)\\\\\nO(\\xi)&amp;M_0|_{\\operatorname{im}M_0}+O(\\xi)\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe lower-right block is invertible, and its Schur complement is \\(\\xi G+O(\\xi^2)\\). Thus the upper-left block of \\(M(\\xi)^{-1}\\) is\n\\[\n\\xi^{-1}G^{-1}+O(1);\n\\]\nits off-diagonal blocks are bounded, and its lower-right block tends to \\(M_0^{-1}|_{\\operatorname{im}M_0}\\).</p><p>The kernel components of both couplings are \\(O(\\xi)\\). Multiplication of these four blocks proves the limit formula.</p><p>For the second assertion, put \\(b=B(0)u\\). Since \\(\\Pi b=0\\), \\(b\\in\\operatorname{im}M_0\\). Solutions of \\(M_0x=b\\) differ by \\(\\ker M_0\\). Projection to \\(eV\\) is an isomorphism on this kernel, so there is a unique solution with \\(ex=0\\). Its \\(f\\)-coordinate is\n\\[\n(fM_0f)^{-1}fb.\n\\]\nAll solutions have the same image under \\(L(0)\\), since \\(L(0)\\) kills the kernel. Comparing this solution with \\(Jb\\) proves the second identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Corollary 7.3, “Equal-size partners.” The proposition also records the functional-linkage and replication conclusions of Section 7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a generic size-\\(p\\) point \\(P\\), linked labels \\(h,h'\\), and a direction \\(v\\) nonzero under every \\(D_h(P;\\cdot)\\), put \\( g_j(\\eta)=\\lambda_j(P+\\eta v)-\\lambda_j(P),\\qquad j=h,h'. \\) For any independent auxiliary …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:\nWork in the spectral setting of Propositions 2–4, with progression \\(S=\\{pN:N\\geq1\\}\\), active spaces \\(T_P\\), partitions \\(\\mathcal P_P\\), and dimensions \\(a_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For a generic size-\\(p\\) point \\(P\\), linked labels \\(h,h'\\), and a direction \\(v\\) nonzero under every \\(D_h(P;\\cdot)\\), put\n\\[\ng_j(\\eta)=\\lambda_j(P+\\eta v)-\\lambda_j(P),\\qquad j=h,h'.\n\\]\nFor any independent auxiliary blocks \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_s\\) of sizes in \\(S\\), let\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(P,C_1,\\ldots,C_s).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Lambda_j^R(\\Delta)\\) is the branch reducing to \\(\\lambda_j(P)\\), then\n\\[\n\\Lambda_h^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_h(P)\n=\n(g_h\\circ g_{h'}^{-1})\n\\bigl(\\Lambda_{h'}^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_{h'}(P)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe univariate series is independent of the auxiliary blocks.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(N\\geq1\\),\n\\[\na_{pN}=Na_p.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There exist \\(r\\in S\\) and \\(K_0\\geq1\\) such that, for independent small generic size-\\(r\\) points\n\\[\nP,Q_1,\\ldots,Q_{K_0},\n\\]\ngeneric fixed arrows \\(U_j:P\\leftarrow Q_j\\) make the loop columns together with\n\\[\nV\\longmapsto m(P,Q_j;U_j,V)\n\\]\nspan \\(T_P\\) after extension to the joint data field. The analogous assertion holds with the other arrow fixed. The same \\(r,K_0\\) work for both orientations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 proves functional linkage; Lemma 5.2 proves replication.</p><p>Choose a finite witness list at size \\(p\\) from Proposition 3, with partner sizes \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_M\\in S\\). Choose \\(r=pN\\) with \\(r\\geq q_j\\) for every \\(j\\). Specialize the size-\\(r\\) base to \\(N\\) independent size-\\(p\\) blocks, using a fresh witness list for each.</p><p>Pad a size-\\(q_j\\) partner to size \\(r\\) by independent generic diagonal data of size \\(r-q_j\\), omitting zero padding. Give both arrows zero entries on the padding. The response remains the original witness on its chosen block and is zero on the others.</p><p>These columns have rank\n\\[\nNa_p=a_r.\n\\]\nAll spectra have maximal count and the necessary disjointness. The regular-minor argument transfers this rank to generic size-\\(r\\) base and partner inputs.</p><p>Assign a separate partner to each paired column. At the witness specialization, fix its forward arrow to the chosen value and test the variable reverse arrow at its witness value. A minor of order \\(a_r\\) is nonzero. Bilinearity makes it nonzero for generic fixed forward arrows and suitable reverse-arrow columns.</p><p>The same argument fixes reverse arrows instead. Increase the number of independent partners to the larger of the two finite numbers, obtaining a common \\(K_0\\). If no paired column is needed, add one unused generic partner so that \\(K_0&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, “Functional linkage.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The derivative \\(g'_{h'}(0)\\) is nonzero, and \\( \\Lambda_h^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_h(P) = (g_h\\circ g_{h'}^{-1}) \\bigl(\\Lambda_{h'}^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_{h'}(P)\\bigr). \\) The univariate series depends only on \\(P,h,h',v\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P\\) be a small generic size-\\(p\\) input, and let \\(h,h'\\) be linked labels. Let \\(v\\) be a constant direction with every \\(D_j(P;v)\\neq0\\). Let \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_s\\), possibly none, be jointly independent small generic inputs of sizes in \\(S\\), independent of \\(P\\), and put\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(P,C_1,\\ldots,C_s).\n\\]\nOver a common splitting field \\(E\\), let \\(\\Lambda_j^R(\\Delta)\\) be the branch with value \\(\\lambda_j(P)\\) at zero. Put\n\\[\ng_j(\\eta)=\\lambda_j(P+\\eta v)-\\lambda_j(P),\n\\qquad j=h,h'.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The derivative \\(g'_{h'}(0)\\) is nonzero, and\n\\[\n\\Lambda_h^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_h(P)\n=\n(g_h\\circ g_{h'}^{-1})\n\\bigl(\\Lambda_{h'}^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_{h'}(P)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe univariate series depends only on \\(P,h,h',v\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 transports the generic spectral field through\n\\[\nP\\longmapsto P+\\eta v,\n\\qquad\n\\Omega_P\\longrightarrow\\Omega_P[[\\eta]],\n\\]\nwith reduction the identity. Nonzero elements have images with invertible constant term, and roots and projections map to their uniquely labeled Taylor deformations.</p><p>Linkedness gives\n\\[\nD_{h'}(P;v)c_h-D_h(P;v)c_{h'}=0\n\\]\nfor every loop response and every coefficient vector of every paired response. Apply the transport coefficientwise in partner coordinates. These are the same identities at the moved input, with moved roots, projections, and \\(D\\)-factors.</p><p>They remain valid in the triangular specializations of Proposition 4: clear the auxiliary resolvent determinants first, and work at each Taylor degree. The bounded number of upper steps permits Laurent arrow entries; Propositions 1 and 3 provide coefficientwise specialization. Thus transfer applies over \\(E((\\eta))\\) with the transported relation.</p><p>Let \\(v_R\\) be \\(v\\) supported in \\(P\\), and put \\(R(\\eta)=R+\\eta v_R\\). Impose\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{h'}^{R(\\eta)}(H)=\\lambda_{h'}(P+\\eta v).\n\\]\nChoose linear input coordinates\n\\[\nH=uv_R+H_{\\mathrm{rest}}.\n\\]\nThe derivative in \\(u\\) at \\(H=0\\) is the unit \\(D_{h'}(P+\\eta v;v)\\). Formal implicit substitution eliminates \\(u\\), with zero constant term in \\(H_{\\mathrm{rest}}\\), giving the quotient\n\\[\nE[[\\eta,H_{\\mathrm{rest}}]]\n\\hookrightarrow E((\\eta))[[H_{\\mathrm{rest}}]].\n\\]</p><p>On this quotient, scalar-block separation makes the fixed-root expression \\(S_{h'}\\) vanish. Induct on degree in \\(H_{\\mathrm{rest}}\\) to prove \\(S_h=0\\). Both vanish at degree zero. If they vanish below degree \\(l\\), Proposition 4 and the transported relation give scalar leading coefficients\n\\[\n[S_j]_l=c_{j,l}\\pi_j\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nD_{h'}(P+\\eta v;v)c_{h,l}\n=\nD_h(P+\\eta v;v)c_{h',l}=0.\n\\]\nThe first factor is nonzero, hence \\(c_{h,l}=0\\).</p><p>Thus the \\(h\\)-branch is fixed too. Indeed, scalar blocks identify the determinant at the fixed root with a unit times the \\(t\\)-th power of the branch shift. The quotient is a domain, so this shift is zero. Regularity in \\(\\eta\\) and the displayed injection descend the equality to \\(E[[\\eta,H_{\\mathrm{rest}}]]\\).</p><p>Finally change coordinates by \\(\\Delta=H+\\eta v_R\\). The \\(h'\\)-equation becomes\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{h'}^R(\\Delta)-\\lambda_{h'}(P)=g_{h'}(\\eta).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\ng'_{h'}(0)=D_{h'}(P;v)\\neq0,\n\\]\neliminate \\(\\eta\\) by composition with \\(g_{h'}^{-1}\\). Substitution in the \\(h\\)-equation gives the claimed identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Proposition 7.2, “Replication of active dimensions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the spectral setting of Proposition 5, for every positive integer \\(N\\), \\( a_{pN}=Na_p. \\)", "statement": "<p>In the spectral setting of Proposition 5, for every positive integer \\(N\\),\n\\[\na_{pN}=Na_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(n=pN\\), choose independent size-\\(p\\) generic inputs \\(P_1,\\ldots,P_N\\), and set\n\\[\nR_1=\\operatorname{diag}(P_1,\\ldots,P_N).\n\\]\nUse \\(x=R_1+\\Delta_1\\) to label the generic size-\\(n\\) spectrum.</p><p>This substitution embeds \\(K[[x]]\\) into \\(E[[\\Delta_1]]\\). Its Taylor coefficients are uniformly integral in \\(\\varepsilon\\); substituting\n\\[\n\\Delta_1=\\varepsilon X-R_1\n\\]\nwith independent \\(X\\) is convergent and recovers the original series at \\(\\varepsilon X\\), where homogeneous expansion detects nonzero series. The embedding extends to fraction fields and to the spectral splitting field \\(\\Omega_x\\), since scalar branches at \\(R_1\\) supply all generic roots. Roots and projections are regular at \\(\\Delta_1=0\\).</p><p>Fix a block \\(i\\) and linked labels \\(h,h'\\) in \\(\\mathcal P_{P_i}\\). Choose a direction \\(v_i\\) supported there with nonzero response on every label. Lemma 5.1 at \\(R_1\\), followed by differentiation, gives proportional loop responses at \\(x\\).</p><p>Adjoin an independent partner \\(Q\\) of any fixed size in \\(S\\). Lemma 5.1 at \\(\\operatorname{diag}(R_1,Q)\\) uses the same univariate series. Perturb opposite input blocks by \\(uU,zV\\). Either arrow alone preserves the spectrum, so the separate first derivatives vanish. The mixed chain rule gives\n\\[\nD_{h'}(x;v_i)m_h(x,Q;U,V)\n-\nD_h(x;v_i)m_{h'}(x,Q;U,V)=0.\n\\]\nBoth \\(D\\)-factors have nonzero reductions.</p><p>This must hold coefficientwise in \\(Q\\). Clear the determinants of the two partner resolvents. At every fixed \\(\\Delta_1\\)-degree, the remaining series in \\(Q\\) has coefficients uniformly bounded below in valuation: branch and projection coefficients do not involve \\(Q\\), and other factors have two prescribed arrow steps and finitely many Taylor insertions. Proposition 1 detects every coefficient by small generic \\(Q\\).</p><p>Injectivity of the embedding of \\(\\Omega_x\\) descends the identity to the generic size-\\(n\\) field. The cleared determinants have nonzero constant terms in \\(Q\\), so cancel them in \\(\\Omega_x[[Q]]\\).</p><p>This holds separately for every partner size, with the same labels and \\(D\\)-factors. Thus, for every \\(c\\in T_x\\), the ratios\n\\[\nc_h/D_h(x;v_i)\n\\]\nare constant on each old partition member in each block. There are \\(Na_p\\) such members, hence \\(a_n\\leq Na_p\\).</p><p>For the reverse inequality, choose finite spanning witnesses from Proposition 3 for each \\(P_i\\), all partners jointly independent. Support every loop direction and both arrows in block \\(i\\). At \\(R_1\\) these responses give the original active space in that block and vanish elsewhere, so their rank is \\(Na_p\\).</p><p>Evaluate the same finite list at \\(R_1+\\Delta_1\\). Branch data and partner resolvents are regular at zero. A smaller generic rank would force all minors of order \\(Na_p\\) to vanish, including their reductions, contrary to the displayed specialization. Hence \\(a_n\\geq Na_p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 8.5, “Compatible dual relations.” The statement includes the differential-rank and common-space conclusions needed subsequently", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist an arbitrarily large \\(b\\in S\\), an integer \\(a=a_b\\), and \\(g\\geq0\\) such that \\( \\rho_b=a-g,\\qquad \\rho_{bM}\\leq Ma-g\\quad(M\\geq1). \\) For each generic size-\\(b\\) point \\(P\\), select one root from every …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Work in the spectral setting of Propositions 2–5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\in S\\), \\(K_0\\geq1\\) have the equal-size attainability property of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho_n\\) be the generic trace, equivalently maximal-count eigenvalue, differential rank.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist an arbitrarily large \\(b\\in S\\), an integer \\(a=a_b\\), and \\(g\\geq0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\rho_b=a-g,\\qquad \\rho_{bM}\\leq Ma-g\\quad(M\\geq1).\n\\]</p><p>For each generic size-\\(b\\) point \\(P\\), select one root from every active-partition part, identify the selected active coordinates with\n\\[\nE_P\\simeq E^a,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nL_P=\\operatorname{im}D_P,\\qquad H_P=E_P/L_P.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\dim H_P=g\\).</p><p>After common scalar extension, the following hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. At a star input with disjoint maximal-count diagonal spectra and arrows only from hubs to leaves, a loop derivative is its loop response. A reverse-arrow derivative on a leaf–hub pair has the joint paired responses at those two blocks and zero elsewhere.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For independent size-\\(b\\) points \\(P,Q\\) and either generic fixed arrow, the variable reverse-arrow response projects surjectively onto each of \\(H_P,H_Q\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For \\(M\\) independent size-\\(b\\) blocks, the collective span of selected-root derivative columns at arbitrary strictly upper-arrow arrays has dimension at most \\(Ma-g\\). The span of all loop and two-block paired columns has dimension exactly \\(Ma-g\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For every finite nonempty independent collection \\(\\{P_i\\}\\), there is a \\(g\\)-dimensional space\n\\[\nG_{\\{P_i\\}}\\subseteq\\bigoplus_i H_{P_i}^*\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li l2\">projection to each \\(H_{P_i}^*\\) is an isomorphism;</p><p class=\"li l2\">it annihilates all joint paired responses, with both arrows arbitrary;</p><p class=\"li l2\">for a fixed generic arrow on \\(P_i,P_j\\), the annihilator of the reverse-arrow image in \\(H_{P_i}^*\\oplus H_{P_j}^*\\) is exactly the restriction of \\(G_{\\{P_i\\}}\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">adjoining independent blocks and restricting recovers the original space after scalar extension.</p><p>For one block, \\(G_{\\{P\\}}=H_P^*\\). All assertions include \\(g=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.4 establish the rank bounds, star formula, surjectivity, and common tangent space.</p><p>Define \\(G_{\\{P_i\\}}\\) as the annihilator of all loop and joint paired columns in selected coordinates. Lemma 6.4 makes its dimension \\(g\\). Annihilation of loops identifies it with a subspace of\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_i H_{P_i}^*.\n\\]</p><p>If one component of an element is zero at vertex \\(i\\), pairing with columns on \\((i,j)\\) and surjectivity onto \\(H_{P_j}\\) force every other component to vanish. Projection to each component is injective, hence an isomorphism by dimension.</p><p>For a fixed generic arrow on \\((i,j)\\), the reverse image in \\(H_{P_i}\\oplus H_{P_j}\\) has dimension at least \\(g\\), because either endpoint projection is onto. It is killed by the \\(g\\)-dimensional restriction of \\(G\\). The annihilator of that restriction has dimension \\(2g-g=g\\); hence it is exactly the reverse image. This gives the exact pairwise annihilator assertion.</p><p>Restriction from a larger collection annihilates all old loop and pair columns. It is injective by projection to any retained vertex, and both spaces have dimension \\(g\\), so it is onto. For one block the assertion is its loop quotient definition. The arguments include \\(g=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, “Derivatives at a star.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The input has the same roots and multiplicities as its diagonal part.", "statement": "<p>Let allowed-size blocks have split semisimple outputs with disjoint spectra and maximal total root count. Add input arrows only from designated hubs to designated leaves, with no arrows between hubs or between leaves.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The input has the same roots and multiplicities as its diagonal part. A loop derivative is its loop response, with zero entries at other blocks.</p><p>For a fixed arrow \\(U\\) between a leaf \\(P\\) and a hub \\(Q\\), the derivative in its reverse arrow \\(V\\) has entries\n\\[\n(m_h(P,Q;U,V))_h,\\qquad\n(m_k(Q,P;V,U))_k\n\\]\nat those two blocks, and zero elsewhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Order leaves before hubs. The input and output are upper triangular with the original diagonal output blocks. Disjoint spectra make the output similar to their direct sum, so it is semisimple with the stated roots.</p><p>Differentiate \\(\\operatorname{tr}(W^j)\\). A closed block path contributing to a reverse-arrow derivative must use that reverse arrow once and its matching forward arrow once. There is no other return in the star. Its contribution is therefore exactly the mixed-arrow contribution at the diagonal input. Loop derivatives use no off-diagonal return.</p><p>Proposition 3 supplies the mixed-response formula. Convert trace derivatives to root derivatives using the invertible Vandermonde conversion with entries\n\\[\njt\\lambda_h^{j-1}.\n\\]\nThe same proof applies to specialized blocks satisfying the stated hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, “A stable upper bound for the differential rank.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition 6, one can choose an arbitrarily large \\(b\\in S\\) and integers \\(a=a_b\\), \\(g\\geq0\\) such that \\( \\rho_b=a-g,\\qquad \\rho_{bM}\\leq Ma-g\\quad(M\\geq1). \\) At a generic size-\\(b\\) point …", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 6, one can choose an arbitrarily large \\(b\\in S\\) and integers \\(a=a_b\\), \\(g\\geq0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\rho_b=a-g,\\qquad \\rho_{bM}\\leq Ma-g\\quad(M\\geq1).\n\\]</p><p>At a generic size-\\(b\\) point, projection to one representative of each active-partition part identifies the active space with a full \\(a\\)-dimensional coordinate space. Its quotient by the loop-response image has dimension \\(g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(N\\geq1\\), put\n\\[\ng_N=Na_r-\\rho_{rN}.\n\\]\nReplication and \\(\\rho_{rN}\\leq a_{rN}\\) give \\(g_N\\geq0\\).</p><p>For \\(N\\geq K_0\\), use \\(K_0\\) of \\(N\\) independent size-\\(r\\) blocks as hubs and the rest as leaves. By Lemma 6.1 and equal-size attainability, the derivative image projects onto all leaf active spaces. Reverse-arrow variables for different leaves are independent. Thus\n\\[\n\\rho_{rN}\\geq(N-K_0)a_r,\\qquad g_N\\leq K_0a_r.\n\\]\nSpecialized rank is bounded by generic rank through formal minors. The finitely many smaller \\(N\\) do not affect boundedness.</p><p>A bounded nonnegative integer sequence has an eventual minimum attained arbitrarily late. Choose such an occurrence \\(N_0\\geq K_0\\), and set\n\\[\ng=g_{N_0},\\qquad\ng_N\\geq g\\ (N\\geq N_0),\\qquad\nb=rN_0,\\qquad a=N_0a_r.\n\\]\nThen \\(a=a_b\\), \\(\\rho_b=a-g\\), and\n\\[\n\\rho_{bM}=Ma-g_{N_0M}\\leq Ma-g.\n\\]\nNo eventual constancy is required.</p><p>The active partition makes representative projection an isomorphism. The loop image has rank \\(\\rho_b\\), so the quotient dimension is \\(g\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, “Surjectivity at both ends of a pair.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The joint paired-response map into \\(H_P\\oplus H_Q\\) projects surjectively to either summand.", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(b=rN_0,a,g\\) as in Lemma 6.2. For a generic size-\\(b\\) point \\(P\\), let\n\\[\nH_P=E_P/\\operatorname{im}D_P\n\\]\nbe the \\(g\\)-dimensional selected-coordinate loop quotient.</p><p>Let \\(P,Q\\) be independent generic size-\\(b\\) points. For a generic fixed arrow \\(U\\) between them, vary its reverse arrow \\(V\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The joint paired-response map into \\(H_P\\oplus H_Q\\) projects surjectively to either summand. This holds for either fixed orientation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Specialize \\(P,Q\\) to diagonal sums of \\(N_0\\) independent size-\\(r\\) points. For each little block in \\(P\\), the \\(N_0\\geq K_0\\) blocks in \\(Q\\), together with components of the fixed generic arrow, supply enough partners to span its active space after loop columns are included.</p><p>Reverse-arrow entries touching distinct little blocks of \\(P\\) vary independently. Compression at a root of one little block sees only entries at that block. Thus loops and paired columns span all\n\\[\nN_0a_r=a\n\\]\nactive coordinates at the \\(P\\)-end.</p><p>The corresponding nonzero minor persists at generic \\(P,Q,U\\). Roots and resolvents are regular at the disjoint diagonal specialization. Passing to the loop quotient gives surjectivity onto \\(H_P\\).</p><p>The same proof works at the \\(Q\\)-end and for the other fixed orientation. The finitely many nonzero-minor conditions hold simultaneously on a nonempty generic set.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, “A common tangent space.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At inputs with this diagonal and arbitrary strictly upper block arrows, the collective span of all selected-root derivative columns has dimension at most \\(Ma-g\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(b,a,g\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\rho_b=a-g,\\qquad \\rho_{bM}\\leq Ma-g.\n\\]\nFix \\(M\\geq1\\) independent generic size-\\(b\\) inputs and one selected root from each active-partition part in each block.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At inputs with this diagonal and arbitrary strictly upper block arrows, the collective span of all selected-root derivative columns has dimension at most \\(Ma-g\\). The span of all loop columns and all two-block paired columns has dimension exactly \\(Ma-g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(R=Ma-g\\). For \\(M=1\\), this is the loop-rank assertion. Suppose \\(M\\geq2\\).</p><p>Use one block as a hub, ordered last. Loops span a space of dimension \\(M(a-g)\\). Modulo it, reverse derivatives project independently onto the \\(M-1\\) leaf quotients by Lemma 6.3. Thus the star rank is at least\n\\[\nM(a-g)+(M-1)g=R.\n\\]\nThe generic upper bound is \\(R\\). Hence at a generic strictly upper-arrow array \\(U\\), the selected derivative rank equals \\(R\\).</p><p>Over the field containing \\(U\\) and diagonal spectral data, let \\(F(H)\\) be the selected-root Taylor shift map under unrestricted input perturbations. Scalar branches exist by Proposition 2. All \\((R+1)\\)-minors of \\(dF\\) vanish, and some \\(R\\)-minor is a unit at zero.</p><p>Use the corresponding \\(R\\) outputs and remaining input coordinates as formal source coordinates \\((y,z)\\). The formal inverse theorem gives\n\\[\nF(y,z)=(y,G(y,z)).\n\\]\nThe vanishing larger minors imply \\(\\partial G/\\partial z=0\\). Characteristic zero gives \\(G(y,z)=G(y)\\).</p><p>At any formal source point where \\(F=0\\), derivative columns lie in the fixed graph\n\\[\n\\{(v,dG(0)v):v\\in E^R\\}.\n\\]\nChanging only upper arrows leaves every root unchanged. Thus derivatives at all such formal shifts lie in one graph, not merely spaces of equal dimension.</p><p>Let \\(J(U)\\) be the selected Jacobian at an upper-arrow array. It is polynomial in \\(U\\): a trace derivative has one differentiated step, and other off-diagonal steps are strictly upper, so a contributing path uses boundedly many upper arrows. Diagonal sums converge. The trace-to-root conversion depends only on fixed diagonal roots, preserving polynomial dependence.</p><p>Every covector killing the graph kills \\(J(U+S)\\) as a formal identity in upper-arrow shifts \\(S\\). Since it is polynomial, substitute \\(S=V-U\\). The covector kills every coefficient of \\(J(V)\\). Their span over the original diagonal field has dimension at most \\(R\\), since scalar extension preserves rank, and contains every specialized derivative.</p><p>Loops occur at the diagonal input. Two-block paired columns occur with only their matching upper arrow present, by Lemma 6.1. Varying both arrows makes the collection orientation-independent. They lie in the common space, and the one-hub star already gives \\(R\\) independent such columns. Equality follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 9.1, “Cyclic compatibility of the error.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every cyclic orbit \\(O\\) of words of length \\(\\ell\\), and every \\(c\\in G\\), \\( \\sum_{\\omega\\in O}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{h\\in I_i} c_{i,h}e_{i,h,\\omega}=0. \\) The sum is over distinct words, also when the word has a …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use the spectral setting of Propositions 2–6, and fix \\(b,a,g\\) and their compatible relation spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_1,\\ldots,P_m\\), \\(m\\geq1\\), be independent generic size-\\(b\\) points.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I_i\\) be the selected labels in block \\(i\\), with \\(|I_i|=a\\), and let\n\\[\nG\\subseteq\\bigoplus_iH_{P_i}^*\n\\]\nbe their compatible relation space.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z(s_1,s_2)\\) have constant term \\(\\operatorname{diag}(P_1,\\ldots,P_m)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose every fixed-root Schur expression \\(S_{i,h}\\), for all labels, vanishes below degree \\(\\ell\\geq1\\). By Proposition 4, write\n\\[\n[S_{i,h}]_\\omega=e_{i,h,\\omega}\\pi_{i,h},\n\\qquad |\\omega|=\\ell.\n\\]\nFor each word, the scalar tuple at each block belongs to its active space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every cyclic orbit \\(O\\) of words of length \\(\\ell\\), and every \\(c\\in G\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\omega\\in O}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{h\\in I_i}\nc_{i,h}e_{i,h,\\omega}=0.\n\\]\nThe sum is over distinct words, also when the word has a nontrivial rotational stabilizer.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 7.3 for arbitrary \\(S_1,S_2\\), using the loops chosen in Lemma 7.1. The loop contribution vanishes: if\n\\[\nY_i=\\sum_\\alpha Y_{i,\\alpha}\\otimes T_{i,\\alpha},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\in I_i}c_{i,h}\\operatorname{tr}(D_{i,h}(Y_i))\n=\n\\sum_\\alpha\\operatorname{tr}(T_{i,\\alpha})\n\\sum_{h\\in I_i}c_{i,h}D_{i,h}(Y_{i,\\alpha})=0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{|\\omega|=\\ell}\n\\left(\\sum_i\\sum_{h\\in I_i}c_{i,h}e_{i,h,\\omega}\\right)\n\\operatorname{tr}(S^\\omega)=0\n\\]\nfor every matrix substitution. Auxiliary scalar extensions impose no restriction because field extension is injective.</p><p>Fix a word\n\\[\n\\omega_0=c_0c_1\\cdots c_{\\ell-1}\n\\]\nand take \\(L=\\ell\\). On vertices modulo \\(\\ell\\), define\n\\[\nS_c=\\sum_{j:c_j=c}E_{j,j+1},\\qquad c=1,2.\n\\]\nFor a word \\(\\omega\\) of length \\(\\ell\\), its trace counts starting vertices where \\(\\omega\\) is read around this directed cycle. It is zero outside the cyclic orbit \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega_0\\), and equals\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell}{|O|}\n\\]\nfor every distinct word in \\(O\\).</p><p>Substitute into the trace identity and cancel this positive integer in characteristic zero. The desired orbit sum follows, including periodic and constant-letter words.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.2, “Splitting repeated blocks.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The loops may be chosen so that every \\(B_{i,h}\\), for all root labels, has simple spectrum.", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 7, fix \\(L\\geq1\\) and arbitrary scalar matrices \\(S_1,S_2\\in M_L(E)\\). Substitute\n\\[\ns_c=\\delta S_c\n\\]\nwith a commuting parameter \\(\\delta\\), and add an order-\\(\\delta^\\ell\\) input loop\n\\[\nY_i\\in M_b(E)^2\\otimes M_L(E)\n\\]\nat each old block. Define\n\\[\nB_{i,h}=\\sum_{|\\omega|=\\ell}e_{i,h,\\omega}S^\\omega+D_{i,h}(Y_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_{i,h}\\) acts entrywise on repeat coefficients.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The loops may be chosen so that every \\(B_{i,h}\\), for all root labels, has simple spectrum. After finite splitting, the repeated leaf output has scalar branches of multiplicity \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_{i,h,j}(\\delta)\n=\n\\lambda_{i,h}+\\delta^\\ell\\theta_{i,h,j}+O(\\delta^{\\ell+1}),\n\\qquad 1\\leq j\\leq L,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta_{i,h,j}\\) are the eigenvalues of \\(B_{i,h}\\).</p><p>Their projections are integral in \\(\\delta\\) and reduce to\n\\[\n\\pi_{i,h}\\otimes\\Pi_{i,h,j},\n\\]\nwith \\(\\Pi_{i,h,j}\\) the rank-one spectral projections of \\(B_{i,h}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Matrix substitution is legitimate coefficientwise in \\(\\delta\\). On the repeated \\((i,h)\\)-space, the leading Schur expression is\n\\[\nI_t\\otimes B_{i,h};\n\\]\nthe old errors give the first sum, and an order-\\(\\delta^\\ell\\) input loop contributes its first response. Lower orders vanish.</p><p>Every \\(D_{i,h}\\) is a nonzero functional, so its matrix-valued extension is onto \\(M_L(E)\\). The discriminant of \\(B_{i,h}\\), as a polynomial in loop coordinates, is not identically zero. The product over finitely many labels is nonzero. Over the infinite field, or an extension, choose loops where it is nonzero. This includes all labels. For \\(L=1\\), the condition is automatic.</p><p>Separate old distinct roots by a formal conjugation equal to identity at zero. The separated \\((i,h)\\)-block is\n\\[\n\\lambda_{i,h}I+\\delta^\\ell(I_t\\otimes B_{i,h})\n+O(\\delta^{\\ell+1}).\n\\]\nTo compare exactly with the fixed-root Schur expression, write \\(M=W-\\lambda_{i,h}I\\) in fixed root/complement blocks \\(M_{uv}\\). If \\(F\\) is the separated root block minus \\(\\lambda_{i,h}I\\), write its invariant columns as\n\\[\n\\binom QR,\\qquad Q=I+O(\\delta),\\quad R=O(\\delta),\n\\qquad\nM\\binom QR=\\binom QR F.\n\\]\nEliminating the lower row gives\n\\[\n(M_{11}-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}M_{21})Q\n=\n(Q-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}R)F.\n\\]\nThe factors relating the Schur expression and \\(F\\) reduce to identity. Thus they have the same first nonzero coefficient, proving the displayed leading block.</p><p>Divide its change from \\(\\lambda_{i,h}I\\) by \\(\\delta^\\ell\\). The constant term has \\(L\\) distinct eigenvalues, so a second integral formal conjugation separates them into size-\\(t\\) blocks.</p><p>Across all old labels there are\n\\[\nmLe_b=e_{bmL}\n\\]\nblocks. Their characteristic polynomials are pairwise coprime over the Laurent fraction field: old roots separate at order zero, and slopes separate within an old root. Proposition 2 bounds the total minimal-polynomial degree by \\(e_{bmL}\\). Each block contributes at least one, so exactly one, and is scalar.</p><p>The two integral conjugations give the asserted integral projections and their limits.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.3, “The limiting derivative columns.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The following columns have finite limits at \\(\\delta=0\\):", "statement": "<p>Use a loop choice from Lemma 7.1. Adjoin \\(L+1\\) independent generic size-\\(b\\) hubs, indexed by \\(p=1,\\ldots,L\\) and \\(*\\), with spectra disjoint from old leaf spectra.</p><p>For each old block \\(i\\):</p><p class=\"li\">hub \\(p\\) sends an arrow \\(U_{i,p}\\) only to repeat position \\(p\\);</p><p class=\"li\">hub \\(*\\) sends the same arrow \\(U_{i,*}\\) to every repeat position;</p><p class=\"li\">reverse arrows are zero at the base point.</p><p>Choose the arrows generically so that Proposition 6 applies. Treat the deformed leaf array as one block before the hubs. Set\n\\[\nN=mL+L+1.\n\\]\nSelect the \\(L\\) branches over every old selected label and the selected hub labels, giving \\(Na\\) rows.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following columns have finite limits at \\(\\delta=0\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A leaf loop \\(Y\\otimes T\\) in old block \\(i\\) has row value\n\\[\nD_{i,h}(Y)\\operatorname{tr}(\\Pi_{i,h,j}T)\n\\]\non \\((i,h,j)\\), and zero elsewhere. Hub loops give their own responses.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For individual hub \\(p\\), a reverse arrow \\(V\\) from repeat position \\(r\\) has values\n\\[\nm_{i,h}(P_i,Q_p;U_{i,p},V)\n\\operatorname{tr}(\\Pi_{i,h,j}E_{pr})\n\\]\non leaf rows, and\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{p=r}\\,m_{p,k}(Q_p,P_i;V,U_{i,p})\n\\]\non hub rows.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For the common hub, replace \\(E_{pr}\\) by \\(\\sum_pE_{pr}\\); the hub response is\n\\[\nm_{*,k}(Q_*,P_i;V,U_{i,*}).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The derivative along the whole input \\(\\delta\\)-curve, multiplied by \\(\\delta^{1-\\ell}\\), has limit \\(\\ell\\theta_{i,h,j}\\) on leaves and zero on hubs.</p><p>A matrix formed from a finite spanning collection of these limiting columns, including the curve column, has rank at most \\(Na-g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Over the Laurent field, the full input is a star with maximal root count. Proposition 6 applies with the whole leaf array as one block.</p><p>For leaf loops, the bounded projection tends to\n\\[\n\\pi_{i,h}\\otimes\\Pi_{i,h,j}.\n\\]\nCompressing the first response and taking trace divided by \\(t\\) gives the first formula.</p><p>For a leaf paired derivative, its Schur inverse is on the hub at \\(\\lambda_{i,h,j}(\\delta)\\), which remains away from the hub spectrum. For a hub derivative, the inverse is on the leaf array at a hub root, which remains away from every old leaf root. These inverses are regular. No inverse of a difference of approaching leaf roots occurs.</p><p>At reduction, the leaf input is diagonal between old blocks and repeated within each. A forward arrow at \\(p\\), followed by a reverse arrow from \\(r\\), inserts \\(E_{pr}\\) on the repeat factor. At the hub end, repeat indices must agree, giving \\(\\mathbf1_{p=r}\\). The paired-response formula, followed by trace divided by \\(t\\), gives the individual-hub formula. Summing forward positions gives the common-hub formula.</p><p>The curve column lies in the span of input derivatives over the Laurent field by the chain rule. Lemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n\\delta^{1-\\ell}\\frac{d}{d\\delta}\\lambda_{i,h,j}(\\delta)\n=\n\\ell\\theta_{i,h,j}+O(\\delta).\n\\]\nHub roots are constant.</p><p>The selected derivative rank is at most\n\\[\n\\rho_{bN}\\leq Na-g\n\\]\nby Proposition 6 and the original trace-minor identities evaluated coefficientwise. Choose finite bases of loop and reverse direction spaces and include the scaled curve column. All larger minors vanish over the Laurent field; finite limits preserve their vanishing. This proves the rank bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.4, “The left nullspace.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the limiting matrix of Lemma 7.2, including the curve column, the left nullspace has dimension \\(g\\).", "statement": "<p>For the limiting matrix of Lemma 7.2, including the curve column, the left nullspace has dimension \\(g\\). Projection to the old blocks identifies it with their compatible relation space \\(G\\).</p><p>For every \\(c\\in G\\), its leaf weights are\n\\[\n\\mu_{i,h,j}=c_{i,h},\n\\]\nindependent of \\(j\\); its hub weights are uniquely determined by compatibility.</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\sum_{h\\in I_i}c_{i,h}\\operatorname{tr}(B_{i,h})=0\n\\qquad(c\\in G).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mu_{i,h,j}\\) be leaf weights and \\(\\nu_p,\\nu_*\\) hub weights in the left nullspace. Put\n\\[\nT_{i,h}=\\sum_{j=1}^L\\mu_{i,h,j}\\Pi_{i,h,j}.\n\\]</p><p>Loop columns imply\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\in I_i}D_{i,h}(Y)\\operatorname{tr}(T_{i,h}T)=0\n\\]\nfor every \\(Y,T\\). Thus each entry vector\n\\[\n((T_{i,h})_{rp})_{h\\in I_i}\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(H_{P_i}^*\\). Hub weights likewise annihilate their loop images.</p><p>Fix individual hub \\(p\\) and \\(r\\neq p\\). There is no hub response. Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(T_{i,h}E_{pr})=(T_{i,h})_{rp},\n\\]\nthe reverse columns say this entry functional kills the leaf endpoint image. Surjectivity in Proposition 6 makes it zero. Varying \\(r,p\\) shows every \\(T_{i,h}\\) is diagonal.</p><p>For \\(r=p\\), the diagonal-entry functional and \\(\\nu_p\\) form exactly the pair relation of Proposition 6.</p><p>For the common hub,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}\\left(T_{i,h}\\sum_pE_{pr}\\right)\n=\n\\sum_p(T_{i,h})_{rp}\n=\n(T_{i,h})_{rr}.\n\\]\nThus every diagonal-entry functional is paired with the same \\(\\nu_*\\). Projection of the pair relation to the hub dual is an isomorphism, so these functionals are independent of \\(r\\). Compatibility across old blocks and hubs gives\n\\[\nT_{i,h}=c_{i,h}I_L,\\qquad c\\in G,\n\\]\nwith uniquely determined hub weights. Orthogonality of the rank-one projections gives \\(\\mu_{i,h,j}=c_{i,h}\\). Hence the nullspace maps injectively into \\(G\\).</p><p>There are \\(Na\\) rows and rank at most \\(Na-g\\), even with the curve column. The nullspace dimension is at least \\(g\\); its injection into the \\(g\\)-dimensional \\(G\\) is therefore an isomorphism. Every \\(c\\in G\\) occurs.</p><p>Pair with the curve column and cancel the nonzero integer \\(\\ell\\):\n\\[\n0=\\sum_i\\sum_{h\\in I_i}\\sum_jc_{i,h}\\theta_{i,h,j}\n=\n\\sum_i\\sum_{h\\in I_i}c_{i,h}\\operatorname{tr}(B_{i,h}).\n\\]\nFor \\(g=0\\), the assertion is vacuous. For \\(L=1\\), there are no off-diagonal entries to eliminate; the common-hub argument still applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 10.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists \\( Z(s_1,s_2)\\in M_{11b}(E)\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^2 \\) with constant term \\(R\\) and exactly the prescribed linear coefficients, such that all fixed-root Schur expressions vanish.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Work over a common split scalar field \\(E\\) in the spectral setting of Propositions 2–7.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(m=11\\), index blocks by \\(\\mathbb Z/11\\mathbb Z\\), take jointly independent generic size-\\(b\\) inputs \\(P_i\\), and put\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(P_i).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">In each block select \\(a\\) labels. Write\n\\[\nD_i:M_b(E)^2\\to E^a,\\qquad\nL_i=\\operatorname{im}D_i,\\qquad H_i=E^a/L_i.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the compatible relation space, with isomorphisms\n\\[\np_i:G\\to H_i^*.\n\\]\nFor each generic fixed arrow \\(U:i\\leftarrow j\\), the joint selected paired responses have annihilator\n\\[\n\\{(p_i(c),p_j(c)):c\\in G\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe generic input-arrow pairs on all edges\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{letter}&amp;i-j\\text{ for }i\\leftarrow j\\\\ \\hline\ns_1&amp;1,2\\\\\ns_2&amp;3,4.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists\n\\[\nZ(s_1,s_2)\\in M_{11b}(E)\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^2\n\\]\nwith constant term \\(R\\) and exactly the prescribed linear coefficients, such that all fixed-root Schur expressions vanish.</p><p>The construction uses finite linear systems. At step \\(\\ell\\geq3\\), new unknowns are degree-\\(\\ell-1\\) coefficients on reverse edges and degree-\\(\\ell\\) coefficients on diagonal blocks.</p><p>If\n\\[\nq(t)=\\prod_{i,h}(t-\\lambda_{i,h})\n\\]\nranges over the distinct constant roots, these Schur equations are equivalent, at every finite word order and in the complete series ring, to\n\\[\nq(W(Z))=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>No forward offset is zero. The sum of two forward offsets lies between \\(2\\) and \\(8\\), hence is nonzero modulo \\(11\\). Therefore no path of one or two forward steps returns to its initial block. Constant steps preserve blocks. The prescribed constant and linear terms, with higher terms zero, solve all Schur equations through degree two.</p><p>Suppose a partial substitution solves below degree \\(\\ell\\geq3\\). Add a correction \\(VB\\) on a reverse edge \\(j\\leftarrow i\\), where \\(|B|=\\ell-1\\), and arbitrary degree-\\(\\ell\\) loops.</p><p>At a block-diagonal constant input, an off-diagonal variation gives an output variation in the same off-diagonal block, so its fixed-root compression is zero. Hence no equation through degree \\(\\ell-1\\) changes.</p><p>At degree \\(\\ell\\), the reverse coefficient must pair with one prescribed linear arrow. Its returning forward edge is unique: reverse offsets \\(10,9,8,7\\) match \\(1,2,3,4\\). If its coefficient is \\(U\\) and color \\(s_c\\), the selected contributions are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{block}&amp;\\text{word}&amp;\\text{response}\\\\ \\hline\ni&amp;s_cB&amp;m_i(U,V)\\\\\nj&amp;Bs_c&amp;m_j(V,U).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThese are the two-arrow Schur responses.</p><p>Previously present degree-at-least-two coefficients cannot interact with a new degree-\\(\\ell-1\\) coefficient in degree \\(\\ell\\). Two new coefficients have degree at least \\(2(\\ell-1)&gt;\\ell\\). Thus the degree-\\(\\ell\\) correction map is linear. Loop corrections give independent \\(D_i\\)-columns at each vertex and word.</p><p>Compute its annihilator. Let \\(f_{i,\\omega}\\in(E^a)^*\\) annihilate every correction. Loop columns imply \\(f_{i,\\omega}\\) kills \\(L_i\\), so uniquely\n\\[\nf_{i,\\omega}=p_i(c_{i,\\omega}),\\qquad c_{i,\\omega}\\in G.\n\\]\nThe paired annihilator identity gives\n\\[\nc_{i,s_cB}=c_{j,Bs_c}\n\\]\non each color-\\(c\\) edge.</p><p>The two offsets of either color are consecutive, say \\(a,a+1\\). Applying the relation to both edges from \\(j\\) yields\n\\[\nc_{j+a,s_cB}=c_{j+a+1,s_cB}.\n\\]\nVarying \\(j\\) makes the value vertex-independent. The edge relation then identifies it with the value on \\(Bs_c\\). Iterating rotations shows that the annihilator is exactly one element of \\(G\\) per cyclic word orbit. Conversely, such assignments annihilate every paired column.</p><p>Proposition 7 says these annihilators kill the degree-\\(\\ell\\) error. Finite-dimensional duality puts the negative error in the correction image. Choose a correction solving all selected equations. Lower degrees remain solved, and Proposition 4 with the active partition forces every nonselected equation to vanish at degree \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>Continue inductively. A coefficient of degree \\(n\\) changes only at steps \\(n,n+1\\), so the series is well-defined. Every Schur coefficient eventually vanishes permanently. This covers \\(g=0\\). For periodic words, the orbit consists of distinct rotations, with no extra multiplicity.</p><p>Lemma 8.1 gives the polynomial equivalence at every finite stage and in the limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(C_h\\) is invertible on the complementary summand, and \\( q(W)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad S_h=0\\text{ for every }h. \\) The equivalence also holds at every finite order, and hence in …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be a field, let \\(\\mathfrak a=(s_1,s_2)\\subset E\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\), and put\n\\[\nT_N=E\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle/\\mathfrak a^{N+1},\n\\qquad N\\geq0.\n\\]\nLet \\(W\\in M_r(T_N)\\) have constant term\n\\[\nW_0=\\sum_{h=1}^e\\lambda_h\\pi_h,\n\\qquad\n\\pi_h\\pi_j=\\delta_{hj}\\pi_h,\\qquad\n\\sum_h\\pi_h=I_r,\n\\]\nwhere the \\(\\lambda_h\\in E\\) are distinct and the \\(\\pi_h\\in M_r(E)\\) are nonzero projections.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nq(t)=\\prod_{h=1}^e(t-\\lambda_h),\\qquad\n\\bar\\pi_h=I_r-\\pi_h,\n\\]\n\\[\nC_h=\\bar\\pi_h(W-\\lambda_hI_r)\\bar\\pi_h,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nS_h=\\pi_h(W-\\lambda_hI_r)\\pi_h\n-\\pi_hW\\bar\\pi_h C_h^{-1}\\bar\\pi_hW\\pi_h.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(C_h\\) is invertible on the complementary summand, and\n\\[\nq(W)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad S_h=0\\text{ for every }h.\n\\]\nThe equivalence also holds at every finite order, and hence in \\(E\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The constant term of \\(C_h\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\neq h}(\\lambda_j-\\lambda_h)\\pi_j,\n\\]\ninvertible on \\(\\bar\\pi_hE^r\\). A matrix over \\(T_N\\) with invertible constant term is invertible: after factoring out the constant term, use a finite geometric series in the augmentation ideal.</p><p>All modules are right \\(T_N\\)-modules, with matrices acting on columns from the left.</p><p>If every \\(S_h=0\\), define\n\\[\nK_hu=u-C_h^{-1}\\bar\\pi_hW\\pi_hu,\n\\qquad u\\in\\pi_hT_N^r.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\pi_hK_hu=u,\\qquad\n(W-\\lambda_hI_r)K_hu=S_hu=0.\n\\]\nThe map \\(K=\\sum_hK_h\\pi_h\\) has constant term \\(I_r\\), so is invertible. Thus\n\\[\nWK=KW_0,\\qquad q(W)=Kq(W_0)K^{-1}=0.\n\\]</p><p>Conversely, suppose \\(q(W)=0\\). Define\n\\[\nP_h=\\prod_{j\\neq h}\\frac{W-\\lambda_jI_r}{\\lambda_h-\\lambda_j}.\n\\]\nThese are unambiguous polynomials in one matrix with central coefficients. Polynomial interpolation modulo \\(q\\) gives spectral idempotents with\n\\[\n(W-\\lambda_hI_r)P_h=0,\\qquad P_h\\equiv\\pi_h\\pmod{\\mathfrak a}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\iota_h:\\pi_hT_N^r\\to T_N^r\\) be inclusion. The endomorphism\n\\[\nA_h=\\pi_hP_h\\iota_h\n\\]\nhas identity constant term and is invertible. Hence\n\\[\n\\widetilde K_h=P_h\\iota_hA_h^{-1}\n\\]\nhas \\(\\pi_h\\widetilde K_h=\\operatorname{id}\\) and\n\\[\n(W-\\lambda_hI_r)\\widetilde K_h=0.\n\\]\nThe complementary component determines\n\\[\n\\bar\\pi_h\\widetilde K_h=-C_h^{-1}\\bar\\pi_hW\\pi_h.\n\\]\nThe remaining component gives \\(S_h=0\\).</p><p>Apply the result modulo each \\(\\mathfrak a^{N+1}\\) to obtain the complete-series assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a central field \\(F_0\\), central division algebras \\( \\widetilde B_0/F_0,\\qquad D_0=A\\otimes_k\\widetilde B_0 \\) with \\(\\deg D_0=dbm\\), and …", "statement": "<p>Division realization and linear descent; this is the construction of Section 11.</p><p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a characteristic-zero field containing every root of unity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/k\\) be a central division algebra of degree \\(d\\), and\n\\[\nw\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle.\n\\]\nLet \\(q_0\\) be the minimal polynomial of \\(w(0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the spectral case \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\), use the block size \\(b\\) from Propositions 6–8. If \\(q_0(w)=0\\), take any \\(b\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(m=11\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a central field \\(F_0\\), central division algebras\n\\[\n\\widetilde B_0/F_0,\\qquad\nD_0=A\\otimes_k\\widetilde B_0\n\\]\nwith \\(\\deg D_0=dbm\\), and\n\\[\nF=F_0((\\varepsilon)),\\qquad\n\\widetilde B=\\widetilde B_0\\otimes_{F_0}F,\\qquad\nD=D_0\\otimes_{F_0}F,\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(D/F\\) is central division.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is\n\\[\nZ\\in\\widetilde B\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^2\n\\]\nwhose split constant term is a diagonal array of \\(m\\) independent small generic size-\\(b\\) pairs.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Its split \\(s_1\\)-coefficient has generic arrow pairs at offsets \\(1,2\\), and its split \\(s_2\\)-coefficient has generic arrow pairs at offsets \\(3,4\\), and no other linear entries.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\), the fixed polynomial\n\\[\nq(t)=\\operatorname{min}_{F}(w(Z(0)))\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(q(w(Z))=0\\). If \\(q_0(w)=0\\), one may take \\(q=q_0\\) and use the initial constant-and-linear substitution without spectral corrections.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Substitution at \\(Z\\) defines a homomorphism\n\\[\nA\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle\n\\longrightarrow\nD\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle.\n\\]\nConsequently it preserves every polynomial identity of any tuple of old series.</p><p>In the spectral case, every correction is obtained by solving a finite affine linear system over \\(F\\); no nonlinear descent is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 9.1–9.2 construct the division algebra, generic constants, arrows, and permitted coefficient spaces.</p><p>Assume first \\(q_0(w)\\neq0\\). Put\n\\[\nW_0=w(P_1^0,P_2^0)\\in D\n\\]\nand let \\(q\\in F[t]\\) be its monic minimal polynomial. It is irreducible because \\(F[W_0]\\) is a finite-dimensional commutative domain, and separable in characteristic zero.</p><p>Its roots are precisely the distinct split eigenvalues of \\(W_0\\). Indeed, left multiplication by \\(W_0\\) has characteristic polynomial a power of \\(q\\), since \\(D\\) is an \\(F[W_0]\\)-vector space. After splitting, left multiplication is a direct sum of copies of the matrix action of \\(W_0\\). Thus every root occurs and there are no other eigenvalues. Separability makes the output semisimple.</p><p>Start with \\(Z^{\\mathrm{init}}\\). Its split Schur equations vanish through degree two, so Proposition 8 gives the same polynomial vanishing.</p><p>Suppose a partial substitution solves \\(q(w(Z))=0\\) through degree \\(\\ell-1\\), where \\(\\ell\\geq3\\). Allow new coefficients in \\(V_{-r}^2\\), \\(r=1,2,3,4\\), at words of length \\(\\ell-1\\), and coefficients in \\(V_0^2\\) at words of length \\(\\ell\\). Impose\n\\[\nq(w(Z))=0\\pmod{(s_1,s_2)^{\\ell+1}}.\n\\]</p><p>This is a finite affine linear system over \\(F\\). Two new insertions have degree at least \\(2(\\ell-1)&gt;\\ell\\). At fixed output degree, there are boundedly many positive-degree insertions; all others are from the \\(\\varepsilon\\)-small constants. Previously fixed coefficients have a finite lower valuation bound. Hence all coefficient sums and linear parts converge in \\(D\\) and are defined over \\(F\\). A finite \\(F\\)-basis of \\(D\\) turns them into ordinary scalar linear equations.</p><p>After scalar extension, the allowed spaces are exactly the reverse edges and loops of Proposition 8. The existing partial solution satisfies its Schur hypotheses by the finite-order polynomial equivalence. Proposition 8 gives a split correction, and the same equivalence gives all polynomial coefficient equations, including preservation of already solved degree-\\(\\ell-1\\) equations.</p><p>Thus the finite system is solvable after field extension. A matrix and augmented matrix have unchanged ranks under scalar extension, so it is solvable over \\(F\\).</p><p>Choose such a correction at every step. Coefficient stabilization gives\n\\[\nZ\\in\\widetilde B\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^2,\n\\qquad q(w(Z))=0,\n\\]\nwith the prescribed constant and linear terms.</p><p>All input coefficients lie in \\(\\widetilde B\\), hence commute with \\(A\\). The same valuation estimate defines a substitution homomorphism on all old series and justifies multiplication and regrouping. Thus every old polynomial identity is preserved.</p><p>If \\(q_0(w)=0\\), use the same construction with arbitrary \\(b\\geq1\\) and \\(Z=Z^{\\mathrm{init}}\\). The substitution homomorphism preserves the old relation, so no spectral corrections are needed.</p><p>In both cases,\n\\[\n\\deg D=11bd&gt;d.\n\\]\nOnly finite linear systems have been descended.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The extension \\(L_0/H_0\\) is cyclic Galois of degree \\(m\\).", "statement": "<p>Division-algebra factors of Section 11.1.</p><p>Let \\(k,A,b,m\\) be as in Proposition 9. Choose independent \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\), put \\(k_0=k(\\alpha,\\beta)\\), and let \\(B_b\\) be the degree-\\(b\\) symbol algebra\n\\[\nu^b=\\alpha,\\qquad v^b=\\beta,\\qquad vu=\\zeta_buv.\n\\]\nFor \\(b=1\\), set \\(B_1=k_0\\). Fix a \\(k_0\\)-basis \\((e_a)_{a=1}^{b^2}\\).</p><p>Introduce jointly independent variables\n\\[\nx_{c,a,j},\\quad y_{r,c,a,j},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nc\\in\\{1,2\\},\\quad 1\\leq a\\leq b^2,\\quad\nj\\in\\mathbb Z/m\\mathbb Z,\\quad r\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(L_0\\) be their rational function field over \\(k_0\\). Let \\(\\sigma\\) increase \\(j\\) by one and fix \\(k_0\\), and put \\(H_0=L_0^{\\langle\\sigma\\rangle}\\).</p><p>For an independent central variable \\(a_*\\), put \\(F_0=H_0(a_*)\\), and form the cyclic algebra \\(C/F_0\\) with coefficient field \\(L_0(a_*)\\) and\n\\[\n\\tau^m=a_*,\\qquad \\tau c\\tau^{-1}=\\sigma(c).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\widetilde B_0=(B_b\\otimes_{k_0}F_0)\\otimes_{F_0}C,\n\\qquad D_0=A\\otimes_k\\widetilde B_0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extension \\(L_0/H_0\\) is cyclic Galois of degree \\(m\\). The algebras \\(C,\\widetilde B_0,D_0\\) are central simple of degrees \\(m,bm,dbm\\), respectively; \\(D_0\\) is division. The Laurent extension \\(D=D_0\\otimes_{F_0}F_0((\\varepsilon))\\) is central division of the same degree.</p>", "proof": "<p>The automorphism \\(\\sigma\\) has order exactly \\(m\\). Artin’s fixed-field theorem gives the cyclic Galois assertion.</p><p>The cyclic algebra has decomposition\n\\[\nC=\\bigoplus_{r=0}^{m-1}L_0(a_*)\\tau^r.\n\\]\nAfter scalar extension containing \\(L_0(a_*)\\), represent \\(c\\in L_0\\) by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma^{-i}(c))_i\n\\]\nand \\(\\tau\\) by the forward cyclic shift with wrap entry \\(a_*\\). These satisfy the defining relations and span all matrix units. Thus \\(C\\) is central simple of degree \\(m\\). Tensor-product and scalar-extension properties give the other degrees.</p><p>By Proposition 2,\n\\[\nD_L=(A\\otimes_kB_b)\\otimes_{k_0}L_0\n\\]\nis division. Extend \\(\\sigma\\) by fixing the old algebra factors. The skew Laurent division ring\n\\[\nD_L((t;\\sigma))\n\\]\nreceives a unital homomorphism from \\(D_0\\) by\n\\[\n\\tau\\longmapsto t,\\qquad a_*\\longmapsto t^m.\n\\]\nThe central element \\(t^m\\) is transcendental over \\(H_0\\). Simplicity makes the map injective; finite dimension makes \\(D_0\\) division.</p><p>The central Laurent argument of Proposition 2 shows that extension by \\(\\varepsilon\\) remains division.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After splitting:", "statement": "<p>Realization of generic blocks, arrows, and correction spaces; Section 11.2.</p><p>Use the algebras and variables of Lemma 9.1, and put \\(F=F_0((\\varepsilon))\\). For \\(r\\in\\mathbb Z/m\\mathbb Z\\), let\n\\[\nV_r=(B_b\\otimes_{k_0}L_0\\,\\tau^r)\\otimes_{H_0}F\n\\subseteq\\widetilde B,\n\\]\nequivalently the \\(F\\)-span of that graded summand.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nP_c^0=\\varepsilon\\sum_a x_{c,a,0}e_a,\\qquad\nU_{r,c}=\\left(\\sum_a y_{r,c,a,0}e_a\\right)\\tau^r,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nZ_c^{\\mathrm{init}}\n=\nP_c^0+(U_{1,c}+U_{2,c})s_1+(U_{3,c}+U_{4,c})s_2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After splitting:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(V_r\\) consists exactly of arbitrary \\(b\\times b\\) entries at block offset \\(r\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\((P_1^0,P_2^0)\\) has the independent small generic diagonal pairs required in Proposition 8;</p><p class=\"li\">the four arrow pairs give jointly independent generic entries on all prescribed edges;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(V_0\\) gives all diagonal corrections, and \\(V_{-r}\\) gives all reverse corrections for offset \\(r\\).</p><p>The independence is over the old split coefficient field, not over \\(H_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Over a splitting field \\(E\\),\n\\[\nL_0\\otimes_{H_0}E\\simeq E^m,\n\\qquad B_b\\otimes E\\simeq M_b(E).\n\\]\nThus \\(V_0\\) gives independent diagonal blocks. Multiplication by \\(\\tau^r\\) moves these to offset \\(r\\), with nonzero wrap factors. This proves the correction-space description.</p><p>First split \\(A,B_b\\) with constants independent of the \\(x,y\\). The \\(m\\) conjugates of each coefficient use disjoint variable sets with distinct index \\(j\\). The split symbol basis is a matrix basis.</p><p>Permuting variables, changing this basis, and multiplying entries by powers of \\(a_*\\) preserve algebraic independence. Hence all generic determinant conditions from the earlier calculations hold simultaneously. Diagonal entries are \\(\\varepsilon\\)-small; arrows need not be small because they have positive free-word degree.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The algebra \\(D\\otimes_FF((\\gamma))\\) is central division.", "statement": "<p>Restoring generation and two transverse directions; Sections 12–12.1 before countable restriction.</p><p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k,A,w\\) satisfy Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nX\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle^u.\n\\]\nWrite \\(X_0\\in A^u\\) for its constant tuple and \\(X_1,X_2\\in A^u\\) for the coefficients of the individual letters.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nJ_A(X_0)=\n\\{([T,X_0^{(1)}],\\ldots,[T,X_0^{(u)}]):T\\in A\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X_0\\) generates \\(A\\) over \\(k\\), and the classes of \\(X_1,X_2\\) in \\(A^u/J_A(X_0)\\) are linearly independent.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(D=D_0\\otimes_{F_0}F\\), \\(F=F_0((\\varepsilon))\\), and the substitution \\(Z\\) of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">For a new central Laurent parameter \\(\\gamma\\), put\n\\[\nX'(s'_1,s'_2)=X(Z(\\gamma+s'_1,s'_2)),\n\\qquad\nw'(s'_1,s'_2)=w(Z(\\gamma+s'_1,s'_2)).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(D\\otimes_FF((\\gamma))\\) is central division. The constant tuple \\(X'_0\\) generates it over \\(F((\\gamma))\\), and the classes of \\(X'_1,X'_2\\) modulo \\(J(X'_0)\\) are linearly independent.</p><p>Every old polynomial identity of \\((X,w)\\), and the relation \\(q(w')=0\\) supplied by Proposition 9, are preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 10.2 to the split diagonal tuple of\n\\[\nY=X(Z).\n\\]\nIt generates the diagonal product. The generic arrow data of Proposition 9 satisfy the transverse edge conditions of that lemma simultaneously: only finitely many nonzero minors are involved, and all use the same independent coordinates as the realization.</p><p>The linear coefficients of \\(Y\\) have exactly the two color supports required by Lemma 10.3. Higher-order corrections do not affect these hypotheses. Thus translation gives full generation and two independent tangent classes after splitting.</p><p>These are finite rank conditions and therefore also hold over the division algebra’s own center. The central Laurent extension by \\(\\gamma\\) preserves division by Proposition 2.</p><p>Both old polynomial identities and the new algebraic relation are preserved by the convergent translation substitution. This proves all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 12.1, “Derivatives of relations.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map taking \\(f\\in I\\) to the coefficients of \\(z_1,z_2\\) in \\(f(X)\\) is onto \\(A^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A/k\\) be finite-dimensional central simple in characteristic zero. Let\n\\[\nX\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle^u\n\\]\nhave \\(X_0\\) generating \\(A\\), with \\(X_1,X_2\\) independent modulo \\(J_A(X_0)\\). Define\n\\[\n\\theta:k\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_u\\rangle\\to A,\n\\qquad \\theta(f)=f(X_0),\\qquad I=\\ker\\theta.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map taking \\(f\\in I\\) to the coefficients of \\(z_1,z_2\\) in \\(f(X)\\) is onto \\(A^2\\). In particular, there are \\(f_1,f_2\\in I\\) with derivative pairs \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Denote the coefficient maps by \\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\). They satisfy\n\\[\n\\delta_j(fg)=\\theta(f)\\delta_j(g)+\\delta_j(f)\\theta(g).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\theta\\) is onto and \\(I\\) is a two-sided ideal,\n\\[\nM=(\\delta_1,\\delta_2)(I)\\subseteq A^2\n\\]\nis an \\(A\\)-bimodule.</p><p>Left and right multiplication generate \\(\\operatorname{End}_k(A)\\). After splitting, left and right matrix units give all endomorphism matrix units on the vector space \\(A\\), and the isomorphism\n\\[\nA\\otimes_kA^{\\mathrm{op}}\\simeq\\operatorname{End}_k(A)\n\\]\ndescends by finite-dimensional linear algebra. Applying these endomorphisms to \\(M\\subseteq A\\otimes_k k^2\\) shows\n\\[\nM=A\\otimes_k V\n\\]\nfor a subspace \\(V\\subseteq k^2\\).</p><p>If \\(V\\neq k^2\\), some nonzero \\((a,b)\\in k^2\\) annihilates it. Then \\(a\\delta_1+b\\delta_2\\) vanishes on \\(I\\) and descends to a derivation of \\(A\\).</p><p>Every \\(k\\)-linear derivation of \\(A\\) is inner. After splitting \\(A=M_d(E)\\), a derivation \\(\\partial\\) is implemented by\n\\[\nT=\\sum_i\\partial(E_{i1})E_{1i}.\n\\]\nDifferentiate \\(E_{kl}E_{i1}=\\delta_{li}E_{k1}\\), multiply by \\(E_{1i}\\), and sum to get\n\\[\n\\partial(E_{kl})=[T,E_{kl}].\n\\]\nThe implementing-element equations on a fixed \\(k\\)-basis are linear over \\(k\\); solvability after extension implies solvability over \\(k\\).</p><p>Hence\n\\[\naX_1+bX_2\\in J_A(X_0),\n\\]\ncontrary to independence. Therefore \\(V=k^2\\), proving surjectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 12.2, “Generic blocks.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each tuple \\( C_i=X(P_i) \\) generates \\(M_{db}\\), and \\( C=\\operatorname{diag}(C_i) \\) generates the full product of these matrix algebras.", "statement": "<p>Under the hypotheses of Lemma 10.1, let \\(d=\\deg A\\), split \\(A\\) into \\(d\\times d\\) matrices, fix \\(b\\geq1\\), and take finitely many independent small generic \\(b\\times b\\) input pairs\n\\[\nP_i=\\varepsilon P_i^\\circ.\n\\]\nWork over a common splitting Laurent field.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each tuple\n\\[\nC_i=X(P_i)\n\\]\ngenerates \\(M_{db}\\), and\n\\[\nC=\\operatorname{diag}(C_i)\n\\]\ngenerates the full product of these matrix algebras.</p><p>For \\(i\\neq j\\), the output derivative of a generic input arrow \\(U=(U_1,U_2)\\) from block \\(j\\) to \\(i\\) is nonzero modulo\n\\[\n\\{C_iT-TC_j:T\\in M_{db}\\}.\n\\]\nMore precisely, the image of all arrow derivatives in this quotient has dimension at least \\(b^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(E\\) contain the splitting data and independent coordinates. Reductions of integral elements in the \\(E((\\varepsilon))\\)-algebra generated by \\(X(P_i)\\) contain \\(M_d(E)\\otimes1\\), since \\(X_0\\) generates \\(A\\).</p><p>Lemma 10.1 gives\n\\[\n\\varepsilon^{-1}f_j(X(P_i))\n\\equiv I_d\\otimes P_{i,j}^\\circ\\pmod{\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nA generic pair generates \\(M_b(E)\\): specialize one matrix to a diagonal matrix with distinct entries and the other to a matrix with every entry nonzero. Interpolation and sandwiching give all matrix units, so the relevant determinant is nonzero.</p><p>The reductions therefore generate\n\\[\nM_d(E)\\otimes M_b(E)=M_{db}(E).\n\\]\nChoose finitely many integral algebra elements whose reductions form a matrix basis. Their determinant is a unit, proving full generation over \\(E((\\varepsilon))\\).</p><p>Representations defined by distinct \\(C_i\\) have distinct kernels, because\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}f_1(C_i)\n=\n\\varepsilon d\\,\\operatorname{tr}P_{i,1}^\\circ+O(\\varepsilon^2)\n\\]\nhas different leading coefficients. Equal kernels would identify their full matrix images while carrying each polynomial evaluation to the corresponding one, and would preserve trace: the trace of left multiplication is matrix size times matrix trace. This contradicts the display.</p><p>The kernels are distinct maximal two-sided ideals. Explicitly, for each \\(i\\neq j\\), choose a polynomial zero in block \\(j\\) and identity in block \\(i\\). Multiplying these over \\(j\\neq i\\) gives identity in block \\(i\\) and zero elsewhere. Multiply by a lift of any matrix to obtain every product coordinate. Thus the diagonal tuple generates the full product.</p><p>At \\(P_i=P_j=0\\), the inner image is\n\\[\nJ_A(X_0)\\otimes M_b,\n\\]\nof dimension \\((d^2-1)b^2\\). The arrow derivative is\n\\[\nX_1\\otimes U_1+X_2\\otimes U_2.\n\\]\nTransverse independence makes the combined rank \\((d^2+1)b^2\\), so generic rank is at least this.</p><p>At a generic pair the inner map has rank at most \\(d^2b^2\\); in fact it is injective, since a nonzero intertwiner between these simple representations would identify their kernels. Subtracting leaves at least \\(b^2\\) arrow directions in the quotient. A generic fixed arrow consequently has nonzero quotient class.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 12.3, “A translation preserving two directions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The constant tuple \\(Y'_0=Y(\\gamma,0)\\) generates \\(M_{mn}(E((\\gamma)))\\), and \\(Y'_1,Y'_2\\) are independent modulo \\(J(Y'_0)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) have characteristic zero, \\(n\\geq1\\), \\(m=11\\), and\n\\[\nY(s_1,s_2)\\in M_{mn}(E)\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^u.\n\\]\nIndex blocks by \\(\\mathbb Z/m\\mathbb Z\\). Suppose:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Y_0=C=\\operatorname{diag}(C_i)\\) generates \\(\\prod_iM_n(E)\\);</p><p class=\"li\">its linear coefficients satisfy\n\\[\n(Y_1)_{ij}=0\\quad\\text{unless }i-j\\in\\{1,2\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n(Y_2)_{ij}=0\\quad\\text{unless }i-j\\in\\{3,4\\};\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">every displayed edge coefficient is nonzero modulo\n\\[\n\\{C_iT-TC_j:T\\in M_n(E)\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For a new central Laurent parameter \\(\\gamma\\), define\n\\[\nY'(s'_1,s'_2)=Y(\\gamma+s'_1,s'_2).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The constant tuple \\(Y'_0=Y(\\gamma,0)\\) generates \\(M_{mn}(E((\\gamma)))\\), and \\(Y'_1,Y'_2\\) are independent modulo \\(J(Y'_0)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The substitution is well-defined. For a fixed new word, a contribution from an old word of length \\(r\\) contains \\(r\\) minus the new length factors of \\(\\gamma\\). Only finitely many terms contribute to each \\(\\gamma\\)-degree. Coefficients converge in \\(E[[\\gamma]]\\), and products are respected.</p><p>Put \\(B=\\prod_iM_n(E)\\), and let \\(I_C\\) be the kernel of free-algebra evaluation at \\(C\\). First-letter differentiation on \\(I_C\\) has a \\(B\\)-bimodule image in \\(M_{mn}(E)\\).</p><p>Its projection onto every first-color edge is nonzero. Otherwise the derivative into that cross bimodule would descend to a derivation\n\\[\n\\delta:B\\to M_n(E)\n\\]\nwith left and right actions from distinct endpoint factors \\(i,j\\). Such a derivation is inner. For the central idempotent \\(e_i\\), differentiation of \\(e_ia=ae_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\delta(a)=a_iT-Ta_j,\\qquad T=\\delta(e_i).\n\\]\nApplied to the generators, this contradicts the edge hypothesis.</p><p>Each full rectangular block is a simple bimodule for its endpoint matrix factors. Thus the derivative image contains the full block on each first-color edge. Divide the corresponding evaluations at \\(Y(\\gamma,0)\\) by \\(\\gamma\\). Their reductions supply these edge blocks. Ordinary evaluations supply the diagonal product. Offset-one edges form a directed cycle, so their products supply every matrix block. Finitely many elements with independent reductions prove full generation.</p><p>For independence of directions, put \\(N=mn\\) and \\(J_0=J(C)\\). The commutant of \\(C\\) consists of \\(m\\) block scalars, so\n\\[\n\\dim J_0=N^2-m.\n\\]\nChoose that many inner columns with independent reductions spanning \\(J_0\\).</p><p>For block scalar \\(c=\\operatorname{diag}(c_iI_n)\\), the integral inner column\n\\[\n\\gamma^{-1}[c,Y(\\gamma,0)]\n\\]\nreduces to \\([c,Y_1]\\). These columns have rank \\(m-1\\) modulo \\(J_0\\): membership \\([c,Y_1]\\in J_0\\) forces \\(c_i=c_j\\) on offset-one edges, and the cycle makes all \\(c_i\\) equal.</p><p>The shifted linear coefficients reduce to \\(Y_1,Y_2\\). Suppose\n\\[\naY_1+bY_2\\in J_0+\\{[c,Y_1]:c\\text{ block scalar}\\}.\n\\]\nAt a second-color edge only \\(bY_2\\) contributes outside \\(J_0\\), so \\(b=0\\). On offset-one edges the relation forces\n\\[\na=c_i-c_j.\n\\]\nSumming around the cycle gives \\(ma=0\\), hence \\(a=0\\).</p><p>There are therefore\n\\[\nN^2-m+(m-1)+2=N^2+1\n\\]\nindependent reductions among inner columns and the two tangents. They lift to independent columns over \\(E((\\gamma))\\). Full generation gives\n\\[\n\\dim J(Y'_0)=N^2-1,\n\\]\nso the two tangent classes are independent.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, “Algebraic lifting.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a countable field extension \\(k'/k\\), a central division algebra \\(A'/k'\\) of strictly larger degree, and …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be countable, of characteristic zero, and contain every root of unity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/k\\) be a finite-dimensional central division algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">In\n\\[\nA\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle,\n\\]\ncoefficients commute with the free letters, multiplication concatenates words and multiplies coefficients in their original order, and the ring is complete for word length.</p><p class=\"li\">For\n\\[\nX\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle^u,\n\\]\nlet \\(X_0\\) be its constant tuple and \\(X_1,X_2\\) its coefficients of the individual letters.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nJ_A(X_0)=\n\\{([T,X_0^{(1)}],\\ldots,[T,X_0^{(u)}]):T\\in A\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X_0\\) generates \\(A\\) as a \\(k\\)-algebra and \\(X_1,X_2\\) have linearly independent classes in \\(A^u/J_A(X_0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w\\in A\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle\\) be arbitrary.</p><p class=\"li\">For a central division algebra \\(B/F\\), its degree is \\(\\sqrt{[B:F]}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a countable field extension \\(k'/k\\), a central division algebra \\(A'/k'\\) of strictly larger degree, and\n\\[\nX'\\in A'\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle^u,\\qquad\nw'\\in A'\\langle\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\rangle\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every noncommutative polynomial identity over \\(k\\) satisfied by \\((X,w)\\) is satisfied by \\((X',w')\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The constant tuple \\(X'_0\\) generates \\(A'\\) over \\(k'\\), and \\(X'_1,X'_2\\) are linearly independent modulo \\(J_{A'}(X'_0)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Some nonzero monic \\(q\\in k'[t]\\) satisfies \\(q(w')=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 constructs a central division algebra of degree \\(11bd&gt;\\deg A\\), a substitution preserving all old polynomial identities, and a monic algebraic relation for the chosen series. If \\(q_0(w)=0\\), use its unconstrained case; otherwise use its spectral correction.</p><p>Proposition 10 translates the first free letter, restoring generation and two transverse directions while preserving the identities and relation.</p><p>It remains to restrict the center to a countable field. In the notation of Proposition 9, put\n\\[\nL=F_0((\\varepsilon))((\\gamma)).\n\\]\nThe field \\(F_0=H_0(a_*)\\) is countable: \\(L_0\\) is generated by finitely many indeterminates over the countable field \\(k(\\alpha,\\beta)\\), and \\(H_0\\subseteq L_0\\).</p><p>Choose an \\(F_0\\)-basis of \\(D_0\\). Let \\(k'\\subseteq L\\) be generated by:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F_0\\);</p><p class=\"li\">the coefficients of \\(q\\), taking \\(q=q_0\\) in the unconstrained case;</p><p class=\"li\">every scalar coordinate, in this basis, of every coefficient of the translated \\(X'\\) and \\(w'\\).</p><p>There are countably many free words and finitely many coordinates per coefficient, so \\(k'\\) is countable. Define\n\\[\nA'=D_0\\otimes_{F_0}k'.\n\\]\nIt is central simple of degree \\(dbm\\). Its extension to \\(L\\) is division, so \\(A'\\) embeds in a division ring and has no zero divisors. Finite dimension makes \\(A'\\) a division algebra.</p><p>All series coefficients and polynomial coefficients lie in this model. Identities descend through injective scalar extension. Generation and transverse independence descend because ranks of their finite coordinate matrices are unchanged by field extension.</p><p>The unconstrained case uses the same generic eleven-block constant and colored linear terms, the same translation, and the same countable restriction. Hence every case increases degree and preserves all required hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The liftings can be scheduled so that a division-ring ultraproduct contains a countable division subring \\( R=k_\\infty[x,y] \\) of characteristic zero satisfying:", "statement": "<p>Countable construction over a central coefficient field; Sections 3.1–3.3 before passage to the actual center.</p><p>Construction and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use the lifting statement of Proposition 11 as in Section 3 of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Start, as stated there, with\n\\[\nk_0=\\mathbb Q,\\qquad A_0=k_0,\\qquad X^{(0)}=(z_1,z_2).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Successive liftings produce countable nested fields \\(k_i\\), central division algebras \\(A_i/k_i\\) of strictly increasing degrees, and pairs\n\\[\nX^{(i)}\\in A_i\\langle\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\rangle^2\n\\]\nsatisfying the generation and transverse-independence hypotheses.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n\\[\n(x_i,y_i)=X_0^{(i)},\\qquad A_i=k_i[x_i,y_i].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(k_\\infty=\\bigcup_i k_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The liftings can be scheduled so that a division-ring ultraproduct contains a countable division subring\n\\[\nR=k_\\infty[x,y]\n\\]\nof characteristic zero satisfying:\n\\[\nk_\\infty\\subseteq Z(R),\\qquad\n\\text{every }r\\in R\\text{ is algebraic over }k_\\infty,\\qquad\n[R:k_\\infty]=\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>No embeddings between the algebras \\(A_i\\) are required.</p>", "proof": "<p>The successive lifting construction uses Proposition 11, with the initialization and iteration stated in Section 3.1. Each lifted constant pair generates its central division algebra, and degrees increase strictly.</p><p>Lemma 12.1 schedules all expressions over all coefficient fields. Lemma 12.2 constructs the countable algebraic division ring over the embedded central field, without requiring algebra embeddings between stages. Lemma 12.3 proves its infinite dimension over that field. These are the asserted conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At stage \\(n\\), lifting \\( w=p_{i_n,j_n}(X^{(n)}) \\) produces a nonzero monic \\( q_{i_n,j_n}(t)\\in k_{n+1}[t] \\) such that \\( q_{i_n,j_n} \\bigl(p_{i_n,j_n}(X^{(n+1)})\\bigr)=0. \\) Every later stage preserves this …", "statement": "<p>Scheduling all polynomial expressions; Section 3.1.</p><p>Use the iterative construction of Proposition 12. When \\(k_i\\) has been constructed, enumerate\n\\[\nk_i\\langle x,y\\rangle=(p_{i,j})_{j\\geq0}.\n\\]\nEnumerate \\(\\mathbb N^2\\) by increasing \\(i+j\\), in a fixed order within each diagonal. Let \\((i_n,j_n)\\) be the \\(n\\)-th pair, beginning with \\(n=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At stage \\(n\\), lifting\n\\[\nw=p_{i_n,j_n}(X^{(n)})\n\\]\nproduces a nonzero monic\n\\[\nq_{i_n,j_n}(t)\\in k_{n+1}[t]\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nq_{i_n,j_n}\n\\bigl(p_{i_n,j_n}(X^{(n+1)})\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nEvery later stage preserves this identity. Consequently, every polynomial expression over every \\(k_i\\) receives an algebraic relation holding at all sufficiently late constant pairs \\((x_h,y_h)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The initial constant pair \\((0,0)\\) generates \\(k_0\\) as a unital \\(k_0\\)-algebra. Its inner-direction space is zero, and its two linear coefficients \\((1,0),(0,1)\\) are independent. The construction invokes the lifting statement successively with these iteration hypotheses.</p><p>The diagonal enumeration has \\(i_n\\leq n\\), so the prescribed expression is defined over \\(k_n\\). Apply Proposition 11 to it.</p><p>The identity\n\\[\nw-p_{i_n,j_n}(X^{(n)})=0\n\\]\nis preserved. The lifted algebraic relation for \\(w\\) therefore gives the displayed relation for the lifted polynomial expression.</p><p>Every later lifting preserves that polynomial identity, now over its coefficient field \\(k_{n+1}\\). Taking constant terms gives it at \\((x_h,y_h)\\) for every \\(h\\geq n+1\\). Every pair \\((i,j)\\) occurs at a finite stage.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The field \\(k_\\infty\\) embeds centrally in \\(\\mathcal D\\).", "statement": "<p>The algebraic ultraproduct subring; Section 3.2.</p><p>Use the fields, algebras, generators, and scheduled relations of Lemma 12.1. Fix a nonprincipal ultrafilter \\(\\mathcal U\\) on \\(\\mathbb N\\), and form\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\prod_{i\\geq0}A_i/\\mathcal U.\n\\]\nFor \\(c\\in k_j\\), use its image in \\(k_i\\subseteq A_i\\) at all \\(i\\geq j\\), with arbitrary earlier coordinates. Let\n\\[\nx=[(x_i)_i],\\qquad y=[(y_i)_i],\\qquad\nR=k_\\infty[x,y]\\subseteq\\mathcal D.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The field \\(k_\\infty\\) embeds centrally in \\(\\mathcal D\\). Every element of \\(R\\) is algebraic over \\(k_\\infty\\). The ring \\(R\\) is a countable division ring of characteristic zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ultraproduct is division: a nonzero class has nonzero coordinates on a set in \\(\\mathcal U\\); use their coordinatewise inverses there.</p><p>The class assigned to \\(c\\in k_j\\) is independent of earlier coordinates and of the choice of \\(j\\). Field operations agree at sufficiently late indices; a nonzero field element remains nonzero there. This defines a central field embedding\n\\[\nk_\\infty\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal D\n\\]\nusing only field inclusions.</p><p>Every \\(r\\in R\\) is \\(p(x,y)\\) for some \\(p\\in k_j\\langle x,y\\rangle\\). Lemma 12.1 supplies a nonzero monic \\(q\\), over a later coefficient field, such that\n\\[\nq(p(x_i,y_i))=0\n\\]\nat every sufficiently late index. Thus \\(q(r)=0\\) in the ultraproduct.</p><p>For \\(0\\neq r\\in R\\), take a nonzero least-degree relation\n\\[\na_0+a_1r+\\cdots+a_nr^n=0,\\qquad a_j\\in k_\\infty.\n\\]\nIts constant coefficient is nonzero; otherwise multiplication by \\(r^{-1}\\) in \\(\\mathcal D\\) gives a shorter relation. Hence\n\\[\nr^{-1}\n=\n-a_0^{-1}(a_1+a_2r+\\cdots+a_nr^{n-1})\\in R.\n\\]\nSo \\(R\\) is division.</p><p>The coefficient field is countable and there are two algebra generators, so \\(R\\) is countable. Its characteristic is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\ell\\geq0\\), some \\(\\ell+1\\) words in \\(x,y\\) are linearly independent over \\(k_\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Infinite dimension over the coefficient field; Section 3.3.</p><p>Use \\(R,k_\\infty,x,y\\) from Lemma 12.2, with\n\\[\nA_i=k_i[x_i,y_i]\n\\]\nand \\([A_i:k_i]\\to\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\ell\\geq0\\), some \\(\\ell+1\\) words in \\(x,y\\) are linearly independent over \\(k_\\infty\\). Therefore\n\\[\n[R:k_\\infty]=\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(V_{i,\\ell}\\) be the \\(k_i\\)-span of words in \\(x_i,y_i\\) of length at most \\(\\ell\\), including \\(1\\).</p><p>If\n\\[\nV_{i,\\ell}=V_{i,\\ell+1},\n\\]\nthen \\(V_{i,\\ell}\\) is stable under right multiplication by \\(x_i,y_i\\), so contains every word and equals \\(A_i\\). Until this occurs, dimensions increase strictly from \\(1\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(\\ell\\), since \\([A_i:k_i]\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_{k_i}V_{i,\\ell}\\geq\\ell+1\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(i\\).</p><p>There are finitely many subsets of size \\(\\ell+1\\) of the finite word list of length at most \\(\\ell\\). The ultrafilter selects one fixed subset \\(w_0,\\ldots,w_\\ell\\) independent on a set in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell}c_jw_j(x,y)=0,\\qquad c_j\\in k_\\infty.\n\\]\nAll coefficients belong to one \\(k_h\\). Equality holds on a set in \\(\\mathcal U\\). Intersect with the independence set and the tail \\(i\\geq h\\). This gives a nonempty set where stage independence forces every \\(c_j=0\\).</p><p>Thus \\(R\\) has arbitrarily large finite independent sets.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 4, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [1, 12, "cite"], [1, 15, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 9, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [4, 12, "cite"], [4, 14, "cite"], [4, 17, "cite"], [4, 21, "cite"], [4, 22, "cite"], [4, 23, "cite"], [4, 26, "cite"], [4, 29, "cite"], [4, 31, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [8, 4, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [9, 17, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [9, 22, "cite"], [9, 26, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [12, 22, "cite"], [12, 26, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 22, "cite"], [14, 26, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 22, "cite"], [17, 24, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [17, 26, "cite"], [17, 28, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 17, "cite"], [22, 26, "cite"], [22, 28, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 22, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [28, 35, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 28, "cite"], [31, 35, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 31, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [35, 37, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 36, "cite"]]});
